1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,462
Chapter 11: First- and Second-Trimester Pregnancy.

3
00:00:06,462 --> 00:00:08,662
Loss Nomenclature.

4
00:00:08,662 --> 00:00:11,862
First-Trimester Spontaneous Abortion.

5
00:00:11,862 --> 00:00:15,550
Spontaneous Abortion Clinical Classification.

6
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:17,800
Recurrent Miscarriage.

7
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:20,300
Second-Trimester Abortion.

8
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,762
Cervical Insufficiency.

9
00:00:22,762 --> 00:00:24,775
Induced Abortion.

10
00:00:24,775 --> 00:00:27,187
First-Trimester Methods.

11
00:00:27,187 --> 00:00:29,625
Second-Trimester Methods.

12
00:00:29,625 --> 00:00:32,150
Postabortal Contraception.

13
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:33,912
References.

14
00:00:33,912 --> 00:00:37,250
Miscarriage is a common event in pregnancy.

15
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:44,050
Most early losses stem from genetic abnormalities, and thus the opportunity for prevention is small.

16
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:51,162
Women with later miscarriage or with recurrent miscarriage more likely have a chronic etiology that may be modified.

17
00:00:51,162 --> 00:00:56,762
In contrast to these spontaneous losses, pregnancy termination may be elected.

18
00:00:56,762 --> 00:01:08,150
For both induced abortion and miscarriage, management options include surgery or medication, and providers should have an understanding of these methods and their potential complications.

19
00:01:08,150 --> 00:01:10,037
Nomenclature.

20
00:01:10,037 --> 00:01:16,550
Abortion is defined as the spontaneous or induced termination of pregnancy before fetal viability.

21
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:20,200
Many prefer miscarriage for spontaneous loss.

22
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:27,525
Induced abortion describes termination with surgery or medication of a live fetus that has not reached viability.

23
00:01:27,525 --> 00:01:31,450
Definitions of an abortus vary among organizations.

24
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:43,937
The National Center for Health Statistics and the World Health Organization define abortion as loss or termination of a pregnancy with a fetus aged younger than 20 weeks’ gestation or weighing <500 g.

25
00:01:43,937 --> 00:01:55,975
These criteria, however, are somewhat contradictory because the mean birthweight of a 20-week fetus approximates 330 g, whereas 500 g is the mean for 22 weeks .

26
00:01:55,975 --> 00:02:02,975
Further confusion may derive from criteria that are set by state laws and define abortion even more widely.

27
00:02:02,975 --> 00:02:07,837
Incongruity also exists for the term early pregnancy loss itself.

28
00:02:07,837 --> 00:02:27,112
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) defines this as a nonviable, intrauterine pregnancy (IUP) within the first 126/7 weeks of gestation that consists of either an empty gestational sac or one containing an embryo or fetus without fetal heart activity.

29
00:02:27,112 --> 00:02:35,112
Recurrent pregnancy loss is variably defined but is meant to identify women with repetitive miscarriage (p. 203).

30
00:02:35,112 --> 00:02:40,162
Other definitions help distinguish intrauterine from ectopic gestations.

31
00:02:40,162 --> 00:02:52,862
The term pregnancy of unknown location (PUL) describes a pregnancy identified by human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) level testing but without a confirmed sonographic location.

32
00:02:52,862 --> 00:03:05,612
In this context, five categories are proposed for early pregnancies: definite ectopic pregnancy, probable ectopic pregnancy, PUL, probable IUP, and definite IUP .

33
00:03:05,612 --> 00:03:11,387
Diagnostic and management options for ectopic gestation are described in Chapter 12.

34
00:03:11,387 --> 00:03:19,887
Last, spontaneous abortion includes subcategories of threatened, incomplete, complete, missed, and inevitable abortions.

35
00:03:19,887 --> 00:03:22,837
These are discussed in the next sections.

36
00:03:22,837 --> 00:03:28,612
Septic abortion is used to further classify any of these that are complicated by infection.

37
00:03:28,612 --> 00:03:31,812
First-Trimester Spontaneous Abortion.

38
00:03:31,812 --> 00:03:34,362
# Pathogenesis.

39
00:03:34,362 --> 00:03:40,350
More than 80 percent of spontaneous abortions occur within the first 12 weeks of gestation.

40
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:47,700
With first-trimester losses, demise of the embryo or fetus nearly always precedes spontaneous expulsion.

41
00:03:47,700 --> 00:03:52,425
Death is usually accompanied by hemorrhage into the decidua basalis.

42
00:03:52,425 --> 00:03:58,800
This is followed by adjacent tissue necrosis that stimulates uterine contractions and expulsion.

43
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:02,925
An intact gestational sac is usually filled with fluid.

44
00:04:02,925 --> 00:04:09,325
Anembryonic miscarriage or preembryonic loss describes the group with no identifiable embryo.

45
00:04:09,325 --> 00:04:12,375
The term blighted ovum is less preferred.

46
00:04:12,375 --> 00:04:23,850
An embryonic miscarriage or embryonic loss displays an embryo without cardiac activity during ultrasound evaluation (Kolte, 2015; Pinar, 2018).

47
00:04:23,850 --> 00:04:29,412
In second-trimester losses, the fetus usually does not die before expulsion.

48
00:04:29,412 --> 00:04:35,187
Thus, other sources for abortion are sought and described on page 213.

49
00:04:35,187 --> 00:04:37,350
# Incidence.

50
00:04:37,350 --> 00:04:41,550
Rates for miscarriage vary according to the study population.

51
00:04:41,550 --> 00:04:49,987
In pregnancies aged 5 to 20 weeks’ gestation, the incidence ranges from 10 to 20 percent and is higher in earlier weeks .

52
00:04:49,987 --> 00:04:57,337
To evaluate rates starting at conception, researchers have tested daily urinary samples for βhCG

53
00:04:57,508 --> 00:05:03,908
in women trying to conceive and found that approximately 20 percent of pregnancies failed very early .

54
00:05:03,858 --> 00:05:11,408
These biochemical pregnancy losses are clinically silent and are identified only by dropping β-hCG levels .

55
00:05:11,408 --> 00:05:17,108
Certain factors influence the clinically apparent miscarriage rate and are described next.

56
00:05:17,108 --> 00:05:22,470
It is unknown if these same factors also affect biochemical pregnancy loss.

57
00:05:22,470 --> 00:05:24,958
# Fetal Factors.

58
00:05:24,958 --> 00:05:32,620
Of all miscarriages, approximately half are euploid abortions, that is, carrying a normal chromosomal complement.

59
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:37,270
The other half of all miscarriages has a chromosomal abnormality.

60
00:05:37,270 --> 00:05:43,458
This percentage appears to persist even from evaluations with newer cytogenetic techniques .

61
00:05:43,458 --> 00:05:56,358
However, outside of research, routine use of chromosomal microarray testing of first-trimester fetal tissues is not endorsed by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020d).

62
00:05:56,358 --> 00:06:06,470
The American Society for Reproductive Medicine (2012) recognize its value only if cytogenetic analysis alters future care of a patient.

63
00:06:06,470 --> 00:06:12,233
Both abortion and chromosomal anomaly rates decline with advancing gestational age .

64
00:06:12,233 --> 00:06:18,695
Of chromosomally abnormal embryos, 75 percent aborts by 8 weeks’ gestation.

65
00:06:18,695 --> 00:06:23,958
The rate of abortion with euploid fetuses peaks at approximately 13 weeks .

66
00:06:23,958 --> 00:06:33,095
Of chromosomal abnormalities, 95 percent are caused by maternal gametogenesis errors, and 5 percent by paternal errors .

67
00:06:33,095 --> 00:06:40,345
Thus, the aneuploid abortion incidence rises dramatically after maternal age exceeds 35 years .

68
00:06:40,345 --> 00:06:47,195
Indeed, maternal age is a primary factor underlying the spontaneous loss of aneuploid fetuses .

69
00:06:47,195 --> 00:06:53,408
To a lesser degree, increasing paternal age is also associated with a greater abortion risk .

70
00:06:53,408 --> 00:06:59,395
Not yet well studied, chromosomal abnormalities in spermatozoa likely play a role .

71
00:06:59,395 --> 00:07:14,433
Most common abnormalities are trisomy, found in 50 to 60 percent; monosomy X, in 9 to 13 percent; and triploidy, in 11 to 12 percent (Jenderny, 2014; Sahoo, 2017).

72
00:07:14,433 --> 00:07:22,858
Trisomies typically result from isolated nondisjunction, and rates rise with maternal age (Boué, 1975).

73
00:07:22,858 --> 00:07:29,995
Trisomies of chromosomes 13, 16, 18, 21, and 22 are most common.

74
00:07:29,995 --> 00:07:35,933
Less often, a trisomy forms from a balanced structural chromosomal rearrangements.

75
00:07:35,933 --> 00:07:43,995
These may originate from either parent and are found in 2 to 4 percent of couples with recurrent pregnancy loss (p. 204).

76
00:07:43,995 --> 00:07:50,845
Monosomy X (45,X) is the single most frequent specific chromosomal abnormality.

77
00:07:50,845 --> 00:07:59,670
This is Turner syndrome, which usually results in miscarriage, but liveborn females are described in Chapter 3 (p. 36).

78
00:07:59,670 --> 00:08:04,933
Conversely, autosomal monosomy is rare and incompatible with life.

79
00:08:04,933 --> 00:08:12,295
Triploidy is an additional haploid set of chromosomes, and cells thus contain a total of 69 chromosomes.

80
00:08:12,295 --> 00:08:17,083
The extra haploid set can be either maternally or paternally derived.

81
00:08:17,083 --> 00:08:26,008
Maternally derived digynic triploidy typically results from a meiosis error and fertilization of a diploid ovum by a normal haploid sperm.

82
00:08:26,008 --> 00:08:36,308
Paternally derived diandric triploidy usually forms from the fertilization of a normal haploid ovum by two haploid spermatozoa and leads to partial molar pregnancy .

83
00:08:36,308 --> 00:08:43,745
Triploid fetuses frequently spontaneously abort early, and the few carried longer are all grossly deformed.

84
00:08:43,745 --> 00:08:46,345
# Maternal Factors.

85
00:08:46,345 --> 00:08:53,133
Medical Disorders In chromosomally normal pregnancy losses, maternal contributions can play a role.

86
00:08:53,133 --> 00:09:04,120
For example, a prominent miscarriage risk is associated with poorly controlled diabetes mellitus, obesity, thyroid disease, and systemic lupus erythematosus.

87
00:09:04,120 --> 00:09:14,245
In these, inflammatory mediators may be an underlying theme to pregnancy loss (Kalagiri, 2016; Sjaarda, 2017).

88
00:09:14,245 --> 00:09:19,958
In chapters on these disorders, the miscarriage rate and attempts to lower it are discussed.

89
00:09:19,958 --> 00:09:29,383
In contrast, thrombophilias are no longer linked to miscarriage (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b).

90
00:09:29,383 --> 00:09:35,658
For women undergoing cancer treatment, direct therapeutic radiation can cause miscarriage.

91
00:09:35,658 --> 00:09:41,458
Suggested safe parameters are found in Chapter 49 (p. 872).

92
00:09:41,458 --> 00:09:47,070
Similarly, the effects of chemotherapy on miscarriage rates are not well defined.

93
00:09:47,070 --> 00:09:55,445
Particularly worrisome are women with an ongoing pregnancy after early exposure to the teratogen methotrexate, described in Chapter

94
00:09:55,612 --> 00:09:58,362
8 (p. 152).

95
00:09:58,312 --> 00:10:07,249
Cancer survivors previously treated with abdominopelvic radiotherapy and now subsequently pregnant may carry a greater risk for miscarriage .

96
00:10:07,249 --> 00:10:13,024
Surgical Procedures The miscarriage risk associated with surgery is not well studied.

97
00:10:13,024 --> 00:10:23,787
But, as discussed in Chapter 49 (p. 867), uncomplicated surgical procedures performed during early pregnancy are unlikely to raise this risk .

98
00:10:23,787 --> 00:10:29,862
If indicated, ovarian tumors can generally be resected without inciting miscarriage.

99
00:10:29,862 --> 00:10:36,187
An exception involves early removal of the corpus luteum or the entire ovary in which it resides.

100
00:10:36,187 --> 00:10:45,612
If performed before 10 weeks’ gestation, supplemental progesterone should be given and is described in Chapter 66 (p. 1170).

101
00:10:45,612 --> 00:10:54,462
Trauma seldom causes first-trimester miscarriage, and although Parkland Hospital is a busy trauma center, this is an infrequent association.

102
00:10:54,462 --> 00:11:01,437
Major trauma—especially abdominal—can cause fetal loss but is more likely as pregnancy advances .

103
00:11:01,437 --> 00:11:08,812
Nutrition Sole deficiency of one nutrient or moderate deficiency of all does not appear to raise miscarriage risks.

104
00:11:08,812 --> 00:11:14,962
Even in extreme cases—for example, hyperemesis gravidarum—abortion is rare.

105
00:11:14,962 --> 00:11:26,412
Dietary quality may play a small role, and some data suggest miscarriage risk may decline in women who eat a diet rich in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and fish .

106
00:11:26,412 --> 00:11:31,762
Unlike obesity, underweight is not associated with a greater miscarriage risk .

107
00:11:31,762 --> 00:11:46,199
With caffeine, reports link heavy intake of approximately five cups of coffee per day—about 500 mg of caffeine—with a slightly greater abortion risk (Cnattingius, 2000; Klebanoff, 1999).

108
00:11:46,199 --> 00:11:54,274
Values vary depending on brewing style, but an 8-ounce cup of coffee contains 80 to 100 mg of caffeine.

109
00:11:54,274 --> 00:11:57,374
Black or green tea has half this dose .

110
00:11:57,374 --> 00:12:12,674
Currently, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020e) concludes consumption of <200 mg/d likely is not a major miscarriage risk and that any associated risk with higher intake is unsettled.

111
00:12:12,674 --> 00:12:20,262
Metaanalyses support an increasing dose-related risk (Chen, 2016; Li, 2015).

112
00:12:20,262 --> 00:12:26,374
Behavioral Factors Approximately 7 percent of pregnant women acknowledge cigarette smoking .

113
00:12:26,374 --> 00:12:33,512
One metaanalysis found a slight dose-related relationship between current smoking and early pregnancy loss .

114
00:12:33,512 --> 00:12:41,624
The serious later risks of persistent smoking on pregnancy outcomes are discussed in Chapter 8 (p. 156).

115
00:12:41,624 --> 00:12:47,362
Alcohol consumption carries miscarriage risk mainly in those with chronic or heavy use .

116
00:12:47,362 --> 00:12:54,562
The potent teratogenic effects in these instances are also discussed in Chapter 8 (p. 149).

117
00:12:54,562 --> 00:13:02,149
Environmental Factors Despite the many infections acquired in pregnancy, these uncommonly cause early miscarriage.

118
00:13:02,149 --> 00:13:10,637
The important maternofetal consequences of specific infections in later pregnancy are discussed in Chapters 67 and 68.

119
00:13:10,637 --> 00:13:22,624
Environmental toxins suggested to have a possible link to miscarriage include bisphenol A, phthalates, polychlorinated biphenyls, and dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane (DDT) .

120
00:13:22,624 --> 00:13:26,774
Even fewer studies implicate occupational exposures.

121
00:13:26,774 --> 00:13:34,624
Data suggest a slight increased miscarriage risk in health-care workers exposed to radiation or antineoplastic drugs .

122
00:13:34,624 --> 00:13:41,499
The National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health publishes guidelines on potentially hazardous drugs .

123
00:13:41,499 --> 00:13:47,224
Evidence implicating occupational anesthetic gases in miscarriage is not robust .

124
00:13:47,224 --> 00:13:52,812
Still, gas scavenging systems and workplace exposure limits are recommended .

125
00:13:52,812 --> 00:13:56,987
# Spontaneous Abortion Clinical Classification.

126
00:13:56,987 --> 00:14:05,549
Threatened Abortion This is defined as bleeding through a closed cervical os in the first 20 weeks of pregnancy and with a live embryo or fetus.

127
00:14:05,549 --> 00:14:10,987
It may portend abortion, or it may be associated with conceptus implantation.

128
00:14:10,987 --> 00:14:19,524
Other bleeding sources to exclude are ectopic pregnancy, cervical infection, and dysplastic or neoplastic cervical lesions.

129
00:14:19,524 --> 00:14:25,874
Almost one fourth of women develop bleeding during early gestation that is attributed to threatened abortion .

130
00:14:25,874 --> 00:14:29,549
Bleeding or spotting may persist for days or weeks.

131
00:14:29,549 --> 00:14:36,874
It may be accompanied by suprapubic discomfort, mild cramps, pelvic pressure, or persistent low backache.

132
00:14:36,874 --> 00:14:42,512
Of symptoms, bleeding is by far the most predictive factor for subsequent pregnancy

133
00:14:42,676 --> 00:14:44,226
loss.

134
00:14:44,176 --> 00:14:50,801
Vaginal bleeding or abdominal pain in early pregnancy should prompt hematocrit and blood type assessment.

135
00:14:50,801 --> 00:15:03,538
One primary goal is to exclude ectopic pregnancy, and strategic use of β-hCG levels and transvaginal sonography (TVS) is outlined in Chapter 12 (p. 222).

136
00:15:03,538 --> 00:15:07,476
A second goal is to determine IUP viability.

137
00:15:07,476 --> 00:15:20,663
With an IUP, the gestational sac—an anechoic fluid collection that represents the exocoelomic cavity—may be seen by 4.5 weeks (Fig. 10-2, p. 177).

138
00:15:20,663 --> 00:15:31,701
At this time, β-hCG levels generally measure 1500 to 2000 mIU/mL (Barnhart, 1994; Timor-Tritsch, 1988).

139
00:15:31,701 --> 00:15:46,638
Connolly and colleagues (2013), however, noted that a threshold as high as 3500 mIU/mL may be needed to identify the gestational sac in some cases that ultimately yield a viable singleton IUP.

140
00:15:46,638 --> 00:15:59,426
One caveat during TVS is that a gestational sac may appear similar to a pseudogestational sac, which is an anechoic intrauterine fluid collection (Fig. 12-3, p. 223).

141
00:15:59,426 --> 00:16:07,013
This pseudosac may be blood derived from a bleeding ectopic pregnancy and is easier to exclude once a yolk sac is seen.

142
00:16:07,013 --> 00:16:14,976
Typically, the yolk sac is visible by 5.5 weeks and with a mean gestational-sac diameter of 10 mm.

143
00:16:14,976 --> 00:16:21,388
Thus, the diagnosis of an IUP should be made cautiously if the yolk sac is not yet seen .

144
00:16:21,388 --> 00:16:27,176
Fetal cardiac activity can typically be detected at 6 to 6.5 weeks.

145
00:16:27,176 --> 00:16:33,026
A subchorionic hematoma may also be seen sonographically with threatened miscarriage .

146
00:16:33,026 --> 00:16:37,851
In general, this collection does not portend a greater miscarriage risk .

147
00:16:37,851 --> 00:16:40,213
Figure 11-1.

148
00:16:40,213 --> 00:16:49,163
In this transvaginal sonogram sagittal view of the uterus, a subchorionic hematoma is seen as a very hypoechoic collection (arrow).

149
00:16:49,163 --> 00:16:56,513
It lies adjacent to the round anechoic gestational sac that contains a nearly 7-week embryo (calipers).

150
00:16:56,513 --> 00:17:00,888
Reproduced with permission from Jason McWhirt, ARDMS.

151
00:17:00,888 --> 00:17:05,376
Once threatened abortion is diagnosed, observation is the norm.

152
00:17:05,376 --> 00:17:10,226
Acetaminophen-based analgesia will help relieve cramping discomfort.

153
00:17:10,226 --> 00:17:22,001
The randomized Progesterone in Spontaneous Miscarriage (PRISM) trial found no advantage to progesterone supplementation to lower miscarriage rates in women with first-trimester bleeding .

154
00:17:22,001 --> 00:17:27,588
Bed rest does not improve outcomes and may cause harms such a deep-vein thrombosis .

155
00:17:27,588 --> 00:17:31,801
We do counsel against intercourse until bleeding subsides.

156
00:17:31,801 --> 00:17:38,738
In unusual cases, bleeding with threatened abortion can lead acute severe anemia or hypovolemia.

157
00:17:38,738 --> 00:17:42,426
Pregnancy evacuation is generally indicated.

158
00:17:42,426 --> 00:17:47,101
Less often, transfusion and further observation is elected.

159
00:17:47,101 --> 00:17:55,188
Even if miscarriage does not follow threatened abortion, later rates of preterm birth and placental abruption are slightly increased .

160
00:17:55,188 --> 00:18:03,413
Weiss and coworkers (2004) noted greater risks for later adverse outcomes if early bleeding was heavy rather than light.

161
00:18:03,413 --> 00:18:13,651
Despite these associations, we typically do not add sonography or other surveillance later in pregnancy solely for a first-trimester diagnosis of threatened abortion.

162
00:18:13,651 --> 00:18:20,113
Incomplete Abortion During miscarriage, the cervix opens and placental separation causes bleeding.

163
00:18:20,113 --> 00:18:28,526
Before 10 weeks’ gestation, the fetus and the placenta are frequently expelled together, but later, they often deliver separately.

164
00:18:28,526 --> 00:18:34,738
Thus, tissue may remain entirely within the uterus or partially extrude through the cervix.

165
00:18:34,738 --> 00:18:41,426
Products lying loosely within the cervical canal can be easily extracted or teased out with ring forceps.

166
00:18:41,426 --> 00:18:49,538
For uterine infection or for hemodynamically unstable women with heavy bleeding, prompt surgical evacuation is performed.

167
00:18:49,538 --> 00:19:00,688
For less urgent cases, three management options are curettage, expectant management, or misoprostol (Cytotec), which is prostaglandin E1 (PGE1) .

168
00:19:00,688 --> 00:19:07,113
With all three, treatment complications such as infection and need for transfusion are infrequent.

169
00:19:07,113 --> 00:19:13,013
However, misoprostol and expectant care can be associated with unpredictable bleeding.

170
00:19:13,013 --> 00:19:20,051
Thus, some women will still require unscheduled curettage, which studies have used as a failure endpoint.

171
00:19:20,051 --> 00:19:32,363
Expectant management of spontaneous incomplete abortion has failure rates that approximate 25 percent in randomized trials (Nielsen, 1999; Trinder, 2006).

172
00:19:32,363 --> 00:19:39,688
Medication therapy carries failure rates of 5 to 30 percent (Shochet, 2012; Trinder,

173
00:19:39,844 --> 00:19:51,781
2006). Many of the studies assessing treatment have used an 800-μg vaginal, a 400-μg sublingual, or a 600-μg oral misoprostol dose.

174
00:19:51,731 --> 00:19:59,106
Last, curettage usually results in a quick resolution that is 95- to 100-percent successful.

175
00:19:59,106 --> 00:20:06,569
However, it is invasive, carries surgical risks, and is not necessary for all women (p. 211).

176
00:20:06,569 --> 00:20:11,019
Complete Abortion At times, the entire pregnancy is expelled.

177
00:20:11,019 --> 00:20:18,294
With completion, bleeding ebbs, and the internal cervical os subsequently closes over the next hour or so.

178
00:20:18,294 --> 00:20:26,556
Patients are encouraged to bring in passed tissue, and a gestation should be documented within blood clots or within a decidual cast .

179
00:20:26,556 --> 00:20:33,106
The latter is the thickened endometrium, in the shape of the uterine cavity, that may slough with miscarriage.

180
00:20:33,106 --> 00:20:35,494
Figure 11-2.

181
00:20:35,494 --> 00:20:38,969
A. Decidual cast from a complete abortion.

182
00:20:38,969 --> 00:20:45,794
B. Opening the cast reveals a clear translucent gestational sac, which confirms the diagnosis.

183
00:20:45,794 --> 00:20:55,969
If a gestational sac is not identified within the expelled tissue, TVS helps differentiate a complete abortion from a threatened abortion or ectopic pregnancy.

184
00:20:55,969 --> 00:21:01,519
Findings of complete abortion include a thin endometrium without a gestational sac.

185
00:21:01,519 --> 00:21:05,531
However, this does not guarantee a recent IUP.

186
00:21:05,531 --> 00:21:18,131
One study evaluated 152 women with heavy bleeding and an empty uterus with endometrial thickness <15 mm. Six percent were subsequently found to have an ectopic pregnancy .

187
00:21:18,131 --> 00:21:30,681
Thus, a complete abortion cannot be surely diagnosed unless: (1) true products of conception are seen grossly or (2) sonography documents first an IUP and then later an empty cavity.

188
00:21:30,681 --> 00:21:36,519
In unclear settings, serial serum β-hCG levels aid clarification.

189
00:21:36,519 --> 00:21:40,206
With complete abortion, these levels drop quickly .

190
00:21:40,206 --> 00:21:42,519
Table 11-1.

191
00:21:42,519 --> 00:21:56,319
Percentage Decline of Initial Serum β-hCG Levels Following Complete Spontaneous Abortion Missed Abortion This describes dead products of conception that have been retained for days or weeks within a uterus with a closed cervical os.

192
00:21:56,319 --> 00:22:03,031
Diagnosis is imperative prior to intervention and avoids interruption of a potentially live IUP.

193
00:22:03,031 --> 00:22:07,881
Again, TVS and β-hCG levels are primary tools.

194
00:22:07,881 --> 00:22:12,006
With the latter, levels that plateau or drop are typical.

195
00:22:12,006 --> 00:22:19,844
With TVS, a 1- to 2-mm embryo adjacent to the yolk sac can be seen at 5 to 6 weeks’ gestation.

196
00:22:19,844 --> 00:22:31,531
As listed in Table 11-2, absence of an embryo in a sac with a mean sac diameter (MSD) ≥25 mm signifies pregnancy loss.

197
00:22:31,531 --> 00:22:39,069
The MSD is calculated by summing the length, width, and height of a gestational sac and dividing the sum by three.

198
00:22:39,069 --> 00:22:55,606
Fetal cardiac activity can typically be detected at 6 to 6.5 weeks, with a crown-rump length (CRL) of 1 to 5 mm, and an MSD of 13 to 18 mm (Goldstein, 1992; Levi, 1990).

199
00:22:55,606 --> 00:23:04,531
A CRL threshold ≥7 mm plus absent cardiac activity also is used to diagnose nonviability .

200
00:23:04,531 --> 00:23:12,681
For cases in which a gestational sac has no embryo or yolk sac, additional time and repeat TVS are recommended.

201
00:23:12,681 --> 00:23:17,494
M-mode should be used to document cardiac activity and measure the rate .

202
00:23:17,494 --> 00:23:27,344
Pulsed Doppler is not used routinely and is reserved for specific diagnostic purposes because of theoretical temperature elevation in exposed fetal tissues .

203
00:23:27,344 --> 00:23:29,694
Table 11-2.

204
00:23:29,694 --> 00:23:37,081
Guidelines for Early Pregnancy Loss Diagnosis Less robust sonographic markers may portend pregnancy failure.

205
00:23:37,081 --> 00:23:43,931
A yolk sac diameter >7 mm in pregnancies <10 weeks’ gestation is one .

206
00:23:43,931 --> 00:24:00,569
The fetal heart rate in the first trimester rises from 110 to 130 beats per minute (bpm) at 6 weeks’ gestation to 160 to 170 bpm at 8 weeks (Achiron, 1991; Rauch, 2009).

207
00:24:00,569 --> 00:24:06,356
A slower heart rate is unfavorable, especially one <85 bpm .

208
00:24:06,356 --> 00:24:12,906
Even with cardiac activity, fetuses with a small MSD may presage embryonic loss.

209
00:24:12,906 --> 00:24:19,156
A difference <5 mm between the MSD and CRL values raises concern .

210
00:24:19,156 --> 00:24:24,081
Last, an irregular gestational sac contour may portend loss .

211
00:24:24,081 --> 00:24:31,369
With embryonic or fetal death confirmed, expectant observation, surgery, or medication is an option.

212
00:24:31,369 --> 00:24:40,156
Nonsurgical options balance less invasiveness against heavier associated bleeding, longer completion times, and lower success rates.

213
00:24:40,156 --> 00:24:53,744
Of options, expectant care underperforms medication or surgery, and failure rates range from 15 to 50 percent (Luise, 2002; Trinder, 2006; Zhang, 2005).

214
00:24:53,744 --> 00:24:55,831
Also, weeks

215
00:24:55,996 --> 00:25:01,721
may pass between pregnancy failure diagnosis and actual spontaneous miscarriage.

216
00:25:01,671 --> 00:25:09,796
As a medication option, misoprostol can be given, and a single 800-μg dose vaginally is a common standard.

217
00:25:09,796 --> 00:25:18,033
It may be repeated once in 1 to 2 days, and one large trial reported that 22 percent of women required a second dose .

218
00:25:18,033 --> 00:25:26,721
Overall, failure rates range from 15 to 40 percent (Petersen, 2013; Trinder, 2006).

219
00:25:26,721 --> 00:25:35,783
Success rates are improved by a 200-mg oral dose of mifepristone (Mifeprex) given 24 hours prior to misoprostol .

220
00:25:35,783 --> 00:25:49,671
In the United States, access to this antiprogestin is limited by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to providers participating in the manufacturer’s Risk Evaluation and Mitigation Strategy (REMS) .

221
00:25:49,671 --> 00:25:56,096
Contraindications mirror those listed in the section describing induced abortion (p. 212).

222
00:25:56,096 --> 00:26:05,808
Inevitable Abortion of a Previable Fetus Either rupture of placental membranes or marked ballooning of membranes into the vagina may lead to inevitable abortion.

223
00:26:05,808 --> 00:26:11,308
The latter is discussed in the cervical insufficiency section (p. 205).

224
00:26:11,308 --> 00:26:20,608
Preterm prelabor rupture of membranes (PPROM) at a previable gestational age complicates 0.5 percent of pregnancies .

225
00:26:20,608 --> 00:26:31,758
Instead, periviable birth is defined as delivery between 200/7 and 256/7 weeks and is discussed in Chapter 45 (p. 785).

226
00:26:31,758 --> 00:26:40,058
Clinically with PPROM, abundant vaginal fluid that pools during sterile speculum examination confirms the diagnosis.

227
00:26:40,058 --> 00:26:44,296
Patient coughing or Valsalva maneuver may accentuate this.

228
00:26:44,296 --> 00:26:57,346
Other diagnostic steps can include pH testing, microscopic examination, and sonographic assessment of amnionic fluid volume, and these are outlined in Chapter 22 (p. 426).

229
00:26:57,346 --> 00:27:05,546
Rupture may be spontaneous, and risks are prior PPROM, prior secondtrimester delivery, and tobacco use .

230
00:27:05,546 --> 00:27:10,871
Iatrogenic fluid leakage can follow amniocentesis or fetal surgery.

231
00:27:10,871 --> 00:27:19,458
In some second-trimester cases, fluid may have collected previously between the amnion and chorion and reflects only chorion leakage.

232
00:27:19,458 --> 00:27:28,496
Spontaneous rupture in the first trimester is nearly always followed by either uterine contractions or infection, and termination is typical.

233
00:27:28,496 --> 00:27:40,521
With second-trimester spontaneous PPROM at a previable age, 40 to 50 percent of women will deliver within the first week, and 70 to 80 percent will do so after 2 to 5 weeks .

234
00:27:40,521 --> 00:27:46,958
Average latency is 2 weeks (Hunter, 2012; Kibel, 2016).

235
00:27:46,958 --> 00:28:02,383
Significant maternal complications attend previable PPROM and include uterine infection, sepsis, placental abruption, postpartum hemorrhage, and retained placenta (Dotters-Katz, 2017b; Waters, 2009).

236
00:28:02,383 --> 00:28:10,571
In one study, 56 percent of women suffered one or more of these complications (van der Marel, 2016).

237
00:28:10,571 --> 00:28:15,258
With significant bleeding or fever, the uterus should be evacuated.

238
00:28:15,258 --> 00:28:21,883
Surgical evacuation or medication options for this vary by gestational age and surgeon skill.

239
00:28:21,883 --> 00:28:28,558
Methods mirror those for second-trimester elective abortion, described later (p. 213).

240
00:28:28,558 --> 00:28:38,258
For cases in which previable delivery is inevitable or elected, neonatal consultation aids decision-making and helps form expectations for the family.

241
00:28:38,258 --> 00:28:47,433
For fetuses with a life-limiting condition, such as extreme prematurity, perinatal palliative care is a strategy that emphasizes comfort .

242
00:28:47,433 --> 00:28:54,671
Care team members can include obstetric and neonatal professionals, chaplaincy, and mental health specialists.

243
00:28:54,671 --> 00:29:00,971
In cases without bleeding or fever, expectant management is an option in the well-counseled patient.

244
00:29:00,971 --> 00:29:07,746
Many will choose termination due to the earlierdescribed maternal risks and tenuous neonatal outcomes.

245
00:29:07,746 --> 00:29:15,108
Early fetal mortality stems from pulmonary hypoplasia, severe intraventricular hemorrhage, and sepsis.

246
00:29:15,108 --> 00:29:30,933
Of those managed expectantly with PPROM at <20 weeks, the percentage of infants who are discharged home ranges from 12 to 23 percent (Hunter, 2012; Sim, 2020; van der Martel, 2016).

247
00:29:30,933 --> 00:29:41,196
Common neonatal morbidities are respiratory distress syndrome, bronchopulmonary hypoplasia or dysplasia, necrotizing enterocolitis, and sepsis.

248
00:29:41,196 --> 00:29:51,133
Overall, prognosis is improved if previable PPROM occurs at a later gestation, latency is longer, and oligohydramnios is absent.

249
00:29:51,133 --> 00:29:56,483
With the last, poor lung development and fetal skeletal deformations can

250
00:29:56,644 --> 00:29:59,681
result from scant amnionic fluid.

251
00:29:59,631 --> 00:30:09,219
If expectant care is elected, initial hospital evaluation, diminished physical activity, and observation for fever or labor are reasonable.

252
00:30:09,219 --> 00:30:17,519
After 24 hours, if there is no bleeding, cramping, or fever, a woman may resume ambulation and is discharged home.

253
00:30:17,519 --> 00:30:22,206
She is instructed to watch for fever, contractions, or bleeding.

254
00:30:22,206 --> 00:30:27,244
Antibiotics are considered and given for 7 days to extend latency .

255
00:30:27,244 --> 00:30:41,844
However, lung-maturing corticosteroids, magnesium sulfate neuroprophylaxis, group B streptococcus antibiotic prophylaxis, cesarean delivery, and tocolytics are not recommended before 22 weeks’ gestation .

256
00:30:41,844 --> 00:30:47,156
Once a viable age is reached, readmission until delivery is our practice.

257
00:30:47,156 --> 00:30:54,044
PPROM care at viable gestational ages is described in Chapter 45 (p. 799).

258
00:30:54,044 --> 00:31:01,244
In procedure-related PPROM, iatrogenic leaks are typically higher in the uterus and tend to self-seal.

259
00:31:01,244 --> 00:31:09,719
For leaks from amniocentesis, management is typically conservative, and brief bedrest yields high pregnancy continuation rates.

260
00:31:09,719 --> 00:31:15,756
For rupture after fetal surgery, investigational treatments can address surgical leaks .

261
00:31:15,756 --> 00:31:25,119
One is an occlusive plug—termed an amniopatch—that forms following intraamnionic instillation of autologous platelets and cryoprecipitate.

262
00:31:25,119 --> 00:31:36,844
Another is amnioinfusion to counter the effects of oligohydramnios but is not robustly supported by current data (Roberts, 2014; van Kempen, 2019).

263
00:31:36,844 --> 00:31:44,606
In subsequent pregnancies, the risk for recurrent preterm birth is great in women with prior previable PPROM.

264
00:31:44,606 --> 00:31:54,694
In one cohort study, 46 percent delivered their next pregnancy before 37 weeks, and 17 percent delivered before reaching 24 weeks .

265
00:31:54,694 --> 00:32:05,644
Septic Abortion With spontaneous or induced abortion, organisms may invade myometrial tissues and extend to cause parametritis, peritonitis, and septicemia.

266
00:32:05,644 --> 00:32:09,819
This may complicate both medication and surgical methods.

267
00:32:09,819 --> 00:32:17,744
Classic clinical findings include fever, lower abdominal pain, uterine tenderness, and foul vaginal discharge.

268
00:32:17,744 --> 00:32:23,106
Vaginal bleeding and sonographic evidence of retained uterine tissue are others.

269
00:32:23,106 --> 00:32:30,656
Urinary tract infection should be excluded by urinalysis, and a complete blood count assesses leukocytosis.

270
00:32:30,656 --> 00:32:35,906
Most bacteria causing septic abortion are part of the normal vaginal flora.

271
00:32:35,906 --> 00:32:42,569
Thus, vaginal culture is not informative, however, we obtain blood cultures for those with sepsis.

272
00:32:42,569 --> 00:32:52,606
Particularly worrisome are severe necrotizing infections and toxic shock syndrome (TSS) caused by group A streptococcus —S pyogenes .

273
00:32:52,606 --> 00:32:58,381
Maternal deaths from Clostridium perfringens or C sordellii also have been described.

274
00:32:58,381 --> 00:33:10,894
With TSS, women show severe endothelial injury, capillary leakage, hemoconcentration, hypotension, tachycardia, and marked leukocytosis but may not be febrile initially.

275
00:33:10,894 --> 00:33:20,856
With septic abortion, management includes prompt administration of broad-spectrum antibiotics as discussed in Chapter 37 (p. 651).

276
00:33:20,856 --> 00:33:25,306
If products are retained, suction evacuation is performed.

277
00:33:25,306 --> 00:33:31,131
Most women respond to this treatment within 1 to 2 days and are discharged when afebrile.

278
00:33:31,131 --> 00:33:35,294
Additional oral antibiotics are likely unnecessary .

279
00:33:35,294 --> 00:33:41,844
In a very few women, severe sepsis syndrome develops, and intensive supportive care is essential.

280
00:33:41,844 --> 00:33:49,244
Although rare, widespread peritonitis despite curettage and clinical decline in the patient should raise concerns.

281
00:33:49,244 --> 00:33:55,994
Imaging that shows intraabdominal free air or air within the uterine wall typically prompts laparotomy .

282
00:33:55,994 --> 00:34:00,194
If the uterus is necrotic, hysterectomy is indicated.

283
00:34:00,194 --> 00:34:10,656
Anti-D Immunoglobulin With spontaneous or induced abortion, 2 percent of D-negative women will become alloimmunized if not provided passive isoimmunization.

284
00:34:10,656 --> 00:34:15,844
With surgical dilation and curettage, this rate may reach 5 percent.

285
00:34:15,844 --> 00:34:29,019
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019e) recommends a 300-μg intramuscular dose of anti-Rho (D) immunoglobulin for all gestational ages.

286
00:34:29,019 --> 00:34:31,831
Doses can also be graduated.

287
00:34:31,831 --> 00:34:43,744
Namely, a 50-μg or 120-ug dose is given for pregnancies ≤12 weeks and a 300-μg one for those ≥13 weeks.

288
00:34:43,744 --> 00:34:48,156
This is administered immediately following surgical evacuation.

289
00:34:48,156 --> 00:34:54,344
For medication abortion or expectant management, the injection is given within 72

290
00:34:54,508 --> 00:34:57,783
hours of pregnancy failure diagnosis.

291
00:34:57,733 --> 00:35:04,820
With threatened abortion, immunoglobulin prophylaxis is controversial because of sparse evidence-based data .

292
00:35:04,820 --> 00:35:11,670
It is reasonable to administer anti-D immunoglobulin for a threatened abortion, and this is our practice.

293
00:35:11,670 --> 00:35:13,920
Recurrent Miscarriage.

294
00:35:13,920 --> 00:35:28,883
Affecting approximately 1 percent of fertile couples, recurrent pregnancy loss (RPL) is classically defined as three or more consecutive pregnancy losses at <20 weeks’ gestation or with a fetal weight <500 g.

295
00:35:28,883 --> 00:35:36,345
However, data show the risk for a subsequent loss to be similar whether a woman has two or three prior miscarriages .

296
00:35:36,345 --> 00:35:48,270
The American Society for Reproductive Medicine (2020) now defines RPL as two or more failed pregnancies confirmed by sonographic or histopathological examination.

297
00:35:48,270 --> 00:35:59,733
Primary RPL refers to multiple losses in a woman who has never delivered a liveborn, and secondary RPL refers to multiple pregnancy losses in a patient with a prior live birth.

298
00:35:59,733 --> 00:36:06,920
Remarkably, chances for a successful pregnancy are >50 percent even after five miscarriages .

299
00:36:06,920 --> 00:36:12,045
RPL evaluation addresses its major causes, described next.

300
00:36:12,045 --> 00:36:14,383
Table 11-3.

301
00:36:14,383 --> 00:36:21,870
Predicted Success Rate of Subsequent Pregnancy According to Age and Number of Previous Miscarriages # Etiology.

302
00:36:21,870 --> 00:36:35,095
Widely accepted causes of RPL include some parental chromosomal abnormalities, antiphospholipid antibody syndrome, specific endocrinopathies, and certain structural uterine abnormalities.

303
00:36:35,095 --> 00:36:55,008
Thus, current RPL evaluation includes karyotyping of both parents; measuring antiphospholipid antibody levels; assessing HbA1c, thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), and prolactin levels; and performing saline-infusion sonography (SIS) or hysterosalpingography .

304
00:36:55,008 --> 00:37:03,083
The timing of recurrent loss can offer clues, and in some women, each miscarriage may occur near the same gestational age .

305
00:37:03,083 --> 00:37:07,783
Approximately 50 percent of women have idiopathic RPL .

306
00:37:07,783 --> 00:37:11,508
# Parental Chromosomal Abnormalities.

307
00:37:11,508 --> 00:37:19,620
In first-trimester RPL, the incidence of genetic abnormalities is significantly lower than in sporadic miscarriage .

308
00:37:19,620 --> 00:37:26,395
A notable exception is a parent that carries a reciprocal translocation or Robertsonian translocation .

309
00:37:26,395 --> 00:37:32,833
Their genesis and reproductive sequelae are discussed in Chapter 16 (p. 316).

310
00:37:32,833 --> 00:37:42,020
These account for only 2 to 4 percent of RPL cases, but karyotyping of both parents is a recognized part of RPL evaluation.

311
00:37:42,020 --> 00:37:55,345
After thorough genetic counseling, couples with an abnormal karyotype can be offered in vitro fertilization (IVF) followed by preimplantation genetic testing (PGT) or offered donor gametes .

312
00:37:55,345 --> 00:38:03,233
However, birth rates with IVF plus PGT are not superior to expectant management for this cause of RPL .

313
00:38:03,233 --> 00:38:12,195
Importantly, in couples with RPL who are chromosomally normal, PGT is not recommended solely for the indication of RPL.

314
00:38:12,195 --> 00:38:15,008
# Anatomical Factors.

315
00:38:15,008 --> 00:38:22,120
Several genital tract abnormalities have been implicated in RPL, but direct linkage is not robust.

316
00:38:22,120 --> 00:38:33,645
According to Devi Wold and associates (2006), 15 percent of women with ≥3 consecutive miscarriages will have a congenital or acquired uterine anomaly.

317
00:38:33,645 --> 00:38:38,720
Thus, SIS and hysteroscopy are primary evaluation tools.

318
00:38:38,720 --> 00:38:47,083
Uterine leiomyomas are common, and some may cause miscarriage, especially if located near the placental implantation site.

319
00:38:47,083 --> 00:38:53,545
However, data suggesting a significant link to RPL are not convincing and poor quality .

320
00:38:53,545 --> 00:39:05,133
The American Society for Reproductive Medicine (2017) notes that hysteroscopic excision of large submucosal leiomyomas in women with RPL can be considered.

321
00:39:05,133 --> 00:39:17,020
Uterine synechiae, often called Asherman syndrome, result from broad destruction of endometrium and subsequent scarring, which can follow uterine curettage or hysteroscopic surgeries.

322
00:39:17,020 --> 00:39:25,208
With SIS, multiple hypoechoic bridging bands are seen to span the endometrial cavity, which is filled with anechoic saline.

323
00:39:25,208 --> 00:39:32,220
Treatment is hysteroscopic adhesiolysis, but success rates are lower with more severe initial disease.

324
00:39:32,220 --> 00:39:38,533
Uterine polyps have been found more frequently in women with RPL, but causality is unclear.

325
00:39:38,533 --> 00:39:46,308
These are seen during hysteroscopy or SIS as a mass lesion extending from the uterine wall into the endometrial cavity.

326
00:39:46,308 --> 00:39:49,258
Application of color Doppler during

327
00:39:49,420 --> 00:39:53,895
SIS classically reveals a single feeder vessel reaching the mass.

328
00:39:53,845 --> 00:39:57,457
Hysteroscopic polypectomy can be considered.

329
00:39:57,457 --> 00:40:04,470
For all these acquired lesions, correction can be considered for those with significant uterine cavity distortion.

330
00:40:04,470 --> 00:40:09,095
However, evidence supporting improved birth rates is not robust .

331
00:40:09,095 --> 00:40:17,257
Moreover, reproductive benefits are balanced against intrauterine adhesions that may form after any intracavitary surgery.

332
00:40:17,257 --> 00:40:22,970
Congenital uterine anomalies often originate from abnormal müllerian duct formation.

333
00:40:22,970 --> 00:40:28,782
Depending on their anatomy, some may raise risks for miscarriage or preterm delivery.

334
00:40:28,782 --> 00:40:33,482
Of these, septate uterus is most closely linked with miscarriage.

335
00:40:33,482 --> 00:40:40,220
Hysteroscopic resection has been associated with improved live birth rates in some but not all studies .

336
00:40:40,220 --> 00:40:42,995
It can be considered for RPL .

337
00:40:42,995 --> 00:40:50,970
Chapter 3 (p. 42) contains a fuller discussion of uterine abnormalities and their other obstetrical effects.

338
00:40:50,970 --> 00:40:53,882
# Immunological Factors.

339
00:40:53,882 --> 00:40:59,657
Miscarriages are more common in women with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) .

340
00:40:59,657 --> 00:41:05,520
Many of these women and also some without SLE carry antiphospholipid antibodies.

341
00:41:05,520 --> 00:41:13,195
These are a family of autoantibodies that bind to phospholipid-binding plasma proteins and are associated with RPL .

342
00:41:13,195 --> 00:41:26,220
As shown in Table 11-4, the antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APS) is defined by these antibodies in combination with various forms of reproductive loss or vascular thrombosis .

343
00:41:26,220 --> 00:41:32,782
Chapter 62 (p. 1114) describes pregnancy loss with APS and treatment.

344
00:41:32,782 --> 00:41:35,170
Table 11-4.

345
00:41:35,170 --> 00:41:43,082
Clinical and Laboratory Criteria for Diagnosis of Antiphospholipid Antibody Syndromea # Endocrine Factors.

346
00:41:43,082 --> 00:41:48,620
Of recurrent miscarriages, 8 to 12 percent are caused by endocrine factors .

347
00:41:48,620 --> 00:41:56,995
First, the well-known abortifacient action of uncontrolled diabetes mellitus is detailed in Chapter 60 (p. 1070).

348
00:41:56,995 --> 00:42:02,232
Optimal periconceptional glycemic control will mitigate many of these losses.

349
00:42:02,232 --> 00:42:08,145
Overt hypothyroidism and severe iodine deficiency also raise miscarriage rates .

350
00:42:08,145 --> 00:42:12,057
Correction with supplementation reverses these actions.

351
00:42:12,057 --> 00:42:18,157
Subclinical hypothyroidism, however, does not appear to increase miscarriage rates .

352
00:42:18,157 --> 00:42:25,382
Antithyroid antibodies are a common associate of subclinical hypothyroidism or overt hypothyroidism.

353
00:42:25,382 --> 00:42:33,995
A recent metaanalysis found positive associations between these antibodies and a greater risk for sporadic and recurrent miscarriages .

354
00:42:33,995 --> 00:42:40,857
However, levothyroxine supplementation in this group does not improve subsequent pregnancy outcomes .

355
00:42:40,857 --> 00:42:48,945
For evaluation of RPL, measuring levels of serum TSH, but not antithyroid antibodies, is reasonable.

356
00:42:48,945 --> 00:42:52,995
Other endocrinopathies are inconclusively implicated.

357
00:42:52,995 --> 00:42:57,595
One is progesterone deficiency caused by a luteal-phase defect.

358
00:42:57,595 --> 00:43:06,082
Progesterone supplementation, compared with placebo, did not improve the live birth rate in those with RPL in one large trial .

359
00:43:06,082 --> 00:43:09,795
Limited data implicate hyperprolactinemia .

360
00:43:09,795 --> 00:43:22,407
Obesity, polycystic ovarian syndrome, and insulin resistance are also linked to RPL (Cavalcante, 2019; Craig, 2002; Mayrhofer, 2020).

361
00:43:22,407 --> 00:43:28,095
However, the interplay between these makes assigning individual causality difficult.

362
00:43:28,095 --> 00:43:30,595
Second-Trimester Abortion.

363
00:43:30,595 --> 00:43:32,907
# Etiology.

364
00:43:32,907 --> 00:43:43,670
The timespan that defines a midtrimester fetal loss extends from the end of the first trimester until the fetus weighs <500 g or gestational age reaches 20 weeks.

365
00:43:43,670 --> 00:43:48,920
The spontaneous loss rate in the second trimester is much lower than in the first.

366
00:43:48,920 --> 00:43:57,332
Unlike earlier miscarriages that frequently stem from chromosomal aneuploidies, causes of these later fetal losses are more diverse .

367
00:43:57,332 --> 00:44:04,132
Their ultimate presentations may be miscarriage, PPROM, or fetal demise prior to labor .

368
00:44:04,132 --> 00:44:10,407
Some second-trimester abortions are induced because of fetal abnormalities or patient choice.

369
00:44:10,407 --> 00:44:12,882
Table 11-5.

370
00:44:12,882 --> 00:44:23,082
Some Causes of Midtrimester Spontaneous Pregnancy Losses Second-trimester losses are subclassified similarly to first-trimester ones (p. 200).

371
00:44:23,082 --> 00:44:31,232
Management is similar in many regards to that used for secondtrimester induced abortion, described on page 214.

372
00:44:31,232 --> 00:44:37,082
One exception is cervical cerclage, which may be employed for cervical insufficiency.

373
00:44:37,082 --> 00:44:40,057
# Cervical Insufficiency.

374
00:44:40,057 --> 00:44:46,282
Previously called incompetent cervix, this is characterized classically by painless cervical

375
00:44:46,444 --> 00:44:49,244
dilation in the second trimester.

376
00:44:49,194 --> 00:44:57,956
It can be followed by prolapse and ballooning of the amnionic membranes into the vagina, and ultimately, expulsion of an immature fetus.

377
00:44:57,956 --> 00:45:01,744
This sequence often repeats in future pregnancies.

378
00:45:01,744 --> 00:45:05,769
Of causes, prior cervical trauma is implicated.

379
00:45:05,769 --> 00:45:15,544
One cohort study of more than 15,000 women with prior cervical conization found a fourfold risk of pregnancy loss before 24 weeks’ gestation .

380
00:45:15,544 --> 00:45:24,119
However, in women solely with prior conization but no prior preterm birth, outcomes are not improved by prophylactic cerclage .

381
00:45:24,119 --> 00:45:32,356
In other instances, abnormal cervical development, including that from diethylstilbestrol (DES), may play a role .

382
00:45:32,356 --> 00:45:42,094
Marfan and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes also carry increased risk of cervical insufficiency (Meijboom, 2006; Spiegel, 2020).

383
00:45:42,094 --> 00:45:54,444
Surgical Indications For women with an unequivocal history of second-trimester painless delivery, prophylactic cerclage placement is an option and reinforces a weak cervix by an encircling suture.

384
00:45:54,444 --> 00:46:02,394
However, some women have a history and clinical findings that make it difficult to verify—classic cervical insufficiency.

385
00:46:02,394 --> 00:46:14,306
In one randomized trial of almost 1300 women, cerclage was found to be beneficial—13 versus 17 percent—to prolong pregnancy past 33 weeks .

386
00:46:14,306 --> 00:46:22,294
The physical finding of early dilation of the internal cervical os and visible membranes is another indicator of insufficiency.

387
00:46:22,294 --> 00:46:32,456
In one systematic review, cerclages that were placed based on such examination findings provided superior perinatal outcomes compared with expectant management .

388
00:46:32,456 --> 00:46:36,656
Last, some use TVS to determine cerclage need.

389
00:46:36,656 --> 00:46:47,444
In those with any prior spontaneous preterm birth, the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2016) recommends transvaginal cervical length screening.

390
00:46:47,444 --> 00:46:54,644
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021) also recommends this screening.

391
00:46:54,644 --> 00:47:06,394
Surveillance extends between 16 and 24 weeks’ gestation, and examinations, described in Chapter 14 (p. 254), are performed every 1 to 4 weeks.

392
00:47:06,394 --> 00:47:14,331
In those without prior preterm birth or in those with multifetal gestation, cervical length is not specifically measured.

393
00:47:14,331 --> 00:47:22,081
The cervix should be viewed during the sonographic anatomical anatomy examination at 18 to 22 weeks’ gestation.

394
00:47:22,081 --> 00:47:31,656
Chapter 45 (p. 794) discusses recommendations for cerclage placement based on cervical length to prevent preterm birth.

395
00:47:31,656 --> 00:47:34,631
# Presurgical Preparation.

396
00:47:34,631 --> 00:47:44,381
Contraindications to cerclage include bleeding, contractions, or ruptured membranes, any of which substantially raise the likelihood of labor and failure.

397
00:47:44,381 --> 00:47:53,331
Prophylactic elective cerclage before dilation is preferable, and timing between 12 and 14 weeks’ gestation allows early intervention.

398
00:47:53,331 --> 00:48:03,756
Still, it avoids surgery in the first trimester, which is when most predestined spontaneous losses occur, and screening for aneuploidy and malformation is completed.

399
00:48:03,756 --> 00:48:10,694
Cervical neoplasia screening in suitable candidates and gonorrhea and chlamydial infection testing are done.

400
00:48:10,694 --> 00:48:13,731
Obvious cervical infection is treated.

401
00:48:13,731 --> 00:48:21,444
At times, the cervix instead is found to be dilated, effaced, or both, and an emergency cerclage is performed.

402
00:48:21,444 --> 00:48:29,919
Notably, in more-advanced pregnancy, the risk of stimulating preterm labor or of rupturing membranes with the surgery is greater.

403
00:48:29,919 --> 00:48:36,669
At Parkland Hospital, cerclage procedures are not done after 23 to 24 weeks’ gestation.

404
00:48:36,669 --> 00:48:44,519
Others, however, describe placement later than this (Caruso, 2000; Terkildsen, 2003).

405
00:48:44,519 --> 00:48:51,444
When outcomes of cerclage are evaluated, women with similar clinical presentations are ideally compared.

406
00:48:51,444 --> 00:49:01,719
In a study of elective cerclage by Owen and associates (2009), approximately a third of women delivered before 35 weeks, and complications were few.

407
00:49:01,719 --> 00:49:10,506
By contrast, in a 10-year review of 75 women undergoing emergency cerclage, only half were delivered after 36 weeks .

408
00:49:10,506 --> 00:49:17,581
Importantly, only 44 percent of those with bulging membranes at the time of cerclage reached 28 weeks.

409
00:49:17,581 --> 00:49:22,944
Terkildsen and associates (2003) had similar experiences.

410
00:49:22,944 --> 00:49:30,856
Our experiences at Parkland Hospital are that emergency cerclage has a high failure rate, and women are counseled accordingly.

411
00:49:30,856 --> 00:49:35,144
If the clinical indication for cerclage is questionable, a

412
00:49:35,308 --> 00:49:37,920
woman may instead be observed.

413
00:49:37,870 --> 00:49:44,420
Most undergo cervical examinations weekly or every 2 weeks to assess effacement and dilation.

414
00:49:44,420 --> 00:49:50,395
Unfortunately, rapid effacement and dilation can develop despite such precautions .

415
00:49:50,395 --> 00:49:59,245
Vaginal Cerclage Of the two vaginal cerclage operations, most use the simpler procedure developed by McDonald (1963) .

416
00:49:59,245 --> 00:50:06,708
The more complicated operation is a modification of the procedure described by Shirodkar (1955) .

417
00:50:06,708 --> 00:50:17,995
When either technique is performed electively, women with a classic history of cervical insufficiency have good outcomes (Caspi, 1990; Kuhn, 1977).

418
00:50:17,995 --> 00:50:26,145
For either vaginal or abdominal cerclage, evidence is insufficient to recommend perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis .

419
00:50:26,145 --> 00:50:30,970
Few data are available and do not support prophylactic tocolysis .

420
00:50:30,970 --> 00:50:33,320
Figure 11-3.

421
00:50:33,320 --> 00:50:37,245
McDonald cerclage procedure for incompetent cervix.

422
00:50:37,245 --> 00:50:46,208
A. Start of the cerclage procedure with a no. 2 monofilament suture being placed in the body of the cervix very near the level of the internal os.

423
00:50:46,208 --> 00:50:52,258
B. Continuation of suture placement in the body of the cervix so as to encircle the os.

424
00:50:52,258 --> 00:50:54,870
C. Encirclement completed.

425
00:50:54,870 --> 00:51:04,795
D. The suture is tightened around the cervical canal sufficiently to reduce the diameter of the canal to 5 to 10 mm, and then the suture is tied.

426
00:51:04,795 --> 00:51:09,220
The effect of the suture placement on the cervical canal is apparent.

427
00:51:09,220 --> 00:51:16,420
A second suture placed somewhat higher may be of value if the first is not in close proximity to the internal os.

428
00:51:16,420 --> 00:51:18,858
Figure 11-4.

429
00:51:18,858 --> 00:51:22,970
Modified Shirodkar cerclage for incompetent cervix.

430
00:51:22,970 --> 00:51:30,945
A. A transverse incision is made in the mucosa overlying the anterior cervix, and the bladder is pushed cephalad.

431
00:51:30,945 --> 00:51:38,158
B. A 5-mm Mersilene tape on a swaged-on or Mayo needle is passed anterior to posterior.

432
00:51:38,158 --> 00:51:44,045
C. The tape is then directed posterior to anterior on the other side of the cervix.

433
00:51:44,045 --> 00:51:48,058
Allis clamps are placed so as to bunch the cervical tissue.

434
00:51:48,058 --> 00:51:54,070
This diminishes the distance that the needle must travel submucosally and aids tape placement.

435
00:51:54,070 --> 00:52:00,583
D. The tape is snugly tied anteriorly, after ensuring that all slack has been taken up.

436
00:52:00,583 --> 00:52:05,820
The cervical mucosa is then closed with continuous stitches of chromic suture.

437
00:52:05,820 --> 00:52:09,520
Regional analgesia is suitable and preferred.

438
00:52:09,520 --> 00:52:14,083
After this, the woman is placed in standard lithotomy position.

439
00:52:14,083 --> 00:52:19,133
The vagina and perineum are cleaned for surgery, and the bladder is drained.

440
00:52:19,133 --> 00:52:27,920
Some operators do not use potentially irritating antiseptic solution if amnionic membranes are exposed and instead use warm saline .

441
00:52:27,920 --> 00:52:35,908
Although steps are described here, a thorough and illustrated review of technique is provided by Hawkins (2017).

442
00:52:35,908 --> 00:52:44,683
For suturing, options include a no. 1 or 2 nylon or polypropylene monofilament suture or 5-mm Mersilene tape.

443
00:52:44,683 --> 00:52:54,070
During placement, the suture is placed as cephalad along the cervical length as possible, is anchored into the dense cervical stroma, yet avoids the bladder.

444
00:52:54,070 --> 00:52:58,408
Two tandem cerclage suture rings are not more effective than one .

445
00:52:58,408 --> 00:53:06,533
Emergency cerclage placement with a thinned dilated cervix is more difficult, and tissue tearing and membrane puncture are risks.

446
00:53:06,533 --> 00:53:12,470
Gentle replacement of the prolapsed amnionic sac back into the uterus can aid suturing.

447
00:53:12,470 --> 00:53:20,358
Options include steep Trendelenburg or filling the bladder with 600 mL of saline through a Foley catheter in the bladder.

448
00:53:20,358 --> 00:53:26,283
However, these steps may carry the cervix cephalad and away from the operating field.

449
00:53:26,283 --> 00:53:31,645
Instead, membranes can be pushed inward by a wide, moist sponge stick.

450
00:53:31,645 --> 00:53:40,483
A Foley catheter can instead be inserted through the cervix, and inflation of the 30-mL balloon can deflect the amnionic sac inward.

451
00:53:40,483 --> 00:53:47,520
The balloon is gradually deflated as the cerclage suture is tightened around the catheter tubing, which is then removed.

452
00:53:47,520 --> 00:53:55,483
With any of these, simultaneous gentle outward traction created by ring forceps placed on the cervical edges may be helpful.

453
00:53:55,483 --> 00:54:03,258
For uncomplicated pregnancies without labor, the cerclage is usually snipped and removed at 37 weeks’ gestation.

454
00:54:03,258 --> 00:54:11,120
This balances the risk of preterm birth against that of cervical laceration from a cerclage in place with labor contractions.

455
00:54:11,120 --> 00:54:18,820
Transvaginally placed cerclages are typically removed even with cesarean delivery to avoid rare long-term foreign-body

456
00:54:18,988 --> 00:54:20,938
complications .

457
00:54:20,888 --> 00:54:30,000
With scheduled cesarean delivery, the cerclage may be removed at 37 weeks or deferred until the time of regional analgesia and delivery.

458
00:54:30,000 --> 00:54:34,788
Again, the risk of labor ensuing before delivery must be considered.

459
00:54:34,788 --> 00:54:44,988
During extraction, particularly with a Shirodkar cerclage or a cerclage using Mersilene tape, analgesia aids patient comfort and adequate visualization.

460
00:54:44,988 --> 00:54:50,963
Transabdominal Cerclage At times, suture at the uterine isthmus is placed abdominally.

461
00:54:50,963 --> 00:55:01,438
Performed less often than transvaginal methods, selected indications include prior transvaginal cerclage failure or severe cervical anatomical defects.

462
00:55:01,438 --> 00:55:07,663
The cerclage is left until childbearing completion, and thus cesarean delivery is required.

463
00:55:07,663 --> 00:55:13,188
With the cerclage in place, conception rates still approximate 75 percent .

464
00:55:13,188 --> 00:55:19,600
If needed for a fetal loss, dilation and evacuation can be performed with the suture in place .

465
00:55:19,600 --> 00:55:27,663
With the procedure, after abdomen entry, sharp dissection in the vesicocervical space allows the bladder to be pushed caudally.

466
00:55:27,663 --> 00:55:33,713
At the level of the internal os, a window is made in free space medial to the uterine vessels.

467
00:55:33,713 --> 00:55:37,513
This avoids vessel compression by the tightened cerclage.

468
00:55:37,513 --> 00:55:41,063
The nearby ureter is identified and avoided.

469
00:55:41,063 --> 00:55:47,450
One end of the ligating suture is passed into the right window, and the other is threaded into the left.

470
00:55:47,450 --> 00:55:51,875
Per surgeon preference, the knot is tied either in the front or back.

471
00:55:51,875 --> 00:55:57,413
The vesicouterine peritoneum is closed with absorbable suture in a running fashion.

472
00:55:57,413 --> 00:56:07,375
In the MAVRIC trial, 111 women with a prior failed vaginal cerclage were randomly assigned to transabdominal, McDonald, or Shirodkar methods.

473
00:56:07,375 --> 00:56:12,125
Almost half of the transabdominal ones were placed prior to conception.

474
00:56:12,125 --> 00:56:21,263
The preterm birth rate before 32 weeks was 8 percent in the transabdominal group and 38 percent in each of the transvaginal cerclage groups .

475
00:56:21,263 --> 00:56:31,563
Of morbidity, rates of bleeding, adjacent organ injury, uterine perforation, and infection can be greater with transabdominal compared with transvaginal methods.

476
00:56:31,563 --> 00:56:38,838
Transabdominal cerclage performs suitably whether laparotomy or laparoscopy is used to place the sutures .

477
00:56:38,838 --> 00:56:46,913
Complications With cerclage in general, PPROM, preterm labor, hemorrhage, or infection is a potential risk.

478
00:56:46,913 --> 00:56:50,388
All are uncommon with prophylactic cerclage.

479
00:56:50,388 --> 00:57:00,500
In the trial by MacNaughton and associates (1993), membrane rupture complicated only 1 of more than 600 procedures done before 19 weeks.

480
00:57:00,500 --> 00:57:07,450
In our view, clinical infection mandates immediate removal of the suture with labor induced or augmented.

481
00:57:07,450 --> 00:57:14,450
Similarly, with imminent abortion or delivery, the suture should be removed to avoid cervical laceration.

482
00:57:14,450 --> 00:57:19,500
Following cerclage, sonographic surveillance does not improve outcomes .

483
00:57:19,500 --> 00:57:24,275
Evidence does not support a subsequent reinforcing cerclage procedure .

484
00:57:24,275 --> 00:57:36,213
Membrane rupture during suture placement or within the first 48 hours after surgery is considered by some to be an indication for cerclage removal to avoid serious fetal or maternal infection .

485
00:57:36,213 --> 00:57:45,388
In those with later PPROM but without infection or labor, options include observation alone or cerclage removal and observation.

486
00:57:45,388 --> 00:57:57,825
In these cases, data are contradictory regarding prolonged gestational latency and infection rates with retention or removal (Jenkins, 2000; Pergialiotis, 2015).

487
00:57:57,825 --> 00:58:04,313
In the absence of infection or labor, we allow cerclage retention with close clinical surveillance.

488
00:58:04,313 --> 00:58:06,325
Induced Abortion.

489
00:58:06,325 --> 00:58:08,675
# Definitions.

490
00:58:08,675 --> 00:58:16,150
The term induced abortion is defined as termination of pregnancy with medication or surgery before fetal viability.

491
00:58:16,150 --> 00:58:26,138
The abortion ratio is the number of abortions per 1000 live births, and abortion rate is the number per 1000 women aged 15 to 44 years.

492
00:58:26,138 --> 00:58:32,550
For 2018, approximately 620,000 elective abortions were reported .

493
00:58:32,550 --> 00:58:43,950
Of these, 78 percent were pregnancies aged ≤9 weeks’ gestation, and 92 percent of abortions were completed before ≤13 weeks.

494
00:58:43,950 --> 00:58:51,550
The abortion ratio was 189 per 1000 live births, and the abortion rate was 11.3.

495
00:58:51,550 --> 00:59:01,363
Therapeutic abortion refers to pregnancy termination for medical reasons, and suitable maternal or fetal indications are described in respective chapters.

496
00:59:01,363 --> 00:59:05,788
In cases of rape or incest, many consider termination.

497
00:59:05,956 --> 00:59:14,931
The most frequent therapeutic indication currently is to prevent birth of a fetus with a significant anatomical, metabolic, or mental deformity.

498
00:59:14,881 --> 00:59:24,718
The term elective abortion or voluntary abortion describes the interruption of pregnancy before viability at the request of the woman, but not for medical reasons.

499
00:59:24,718 --> 00:59:27,943
Most abortions done today are elective.

500
00:59:27,943 --> 00:59:30,481
# Legal Influence.

501
00:59:30,481 --> 00:59:37,481
The legality of elective abortion was established by the United States Supreme Court in the case of Roe v. Wade.

502
00:59:37,481 --> 00:59:46,718
The Court defined the extent to which states might regulate abortion and ruled that first-trimester procedures must be left to the medical judgment of the physician.

503
00:59:46,718 --> 00:59:53,231
After this, the state could regulate abortion procedures in ways reasonably related to maternal health.

504
00:59:53,231 --> 01:00:03,481
Last, subsequent to viability, the state could promote its interest in the potential of human life and regulate abortion, except for preservation of the mother’s health.

505
01:00:03,481 --> 01:00:05,956
Other legislation followed.

506
01:00:05,956 --> 01:00:16,631
The 1976 Hyde Amendment forbids use of federal funds to provide abortion services except in cases of rape, incest, or life-threatening circumstances.

507
01:00:16,631 --> 01:00:30,406
The Supreme Court in 1992 reviewed Planned Parenthood v. Casey and upheld the fundamental right to abortion, but established that regulations are constitutional as long as they do not impose an “undue burden” on the woman.

508
01:00:30,406 --> 01:00:40,881
Subsequently, many states introduced counseling requirements, waiting periods, parental consent for minors, facility requirements, and funding restrictions.

509
01:00:40,881 --> 01:00:47,006
Such limits are often called targeted regulation of abortion providers (TRAP) laws.

510
01:00:47,006 --> 01:00:57,943
One major choice-limiting decision was the 2007 Supreme Court decision that reviewed Gonzales v. Carhart and upheld the 2003 Partial-Birth Abortion Ban Act.

511
01:00:57,943 --> 01:01:06,231
In 2016, some TRAP laws were dialed back by the Supreme Court ruling in the case of Whole Woman’s Health v. Hellerstedt.

512
01:01:06,231 --> 01:01:13,856
With this, the justices noted that abortion laws must confer health safety benefits that outweigh burdens on access.

513
01:01:13,856 --> 01:01:16,681
# Provider Availability.

514
01:01:16,681 --> 01:01:22,318
Major women’s health organizations support the legal right of women to obtain an abortion .

515
01:01:22,318 --> 01:01:33,481
The Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education mandates that obstetrics and gynecology residency education must include access to experience with induced abortion.

516
01:01:33,481 --> 01:01:43,431
The Kenneth J. Ryan Residency Training Program was established in 1999 to work with residency programs to improve abortion and contraceptive training.

517
01:01:43,431 --> 01:01:50,731
Moreover, postresidency training in these techniques is available in formal 2-year Family Planning fellowships.

518
01:01:50,731 --> 01:01:55,218
Other residency programs have a less-codified abortion curriculum.

519
01:01:55,218 --> 01:02:03,543
Instead, residents learn technical aspects through their management of miscarriage and pregnancy interruption for medical indications.

520
01:02:03,543 --> 01:02:15,893
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019g) respects the need and responsibility of health-care providers to determine their individual positions on induced abortion.

521
01:02:15,893 --> 01:02:23,731
It also advocates for counseling and timely referral if providers have individual beliefs that preclude pregnancy termination.

522
01:02:23,731 --> 01:02:31,868
Knowledgeable and compassionate counseling objectively describes and provides information to the woman to permit informed decision-making.

523
01:02:31,868 --> 01:02:34,281
First-Trimester Methods.

524
01:02:34,281 --> 01:02:38,456
Abortions can be completed with either medication or surgery.

525
01:02:38,456 --> 01:02:45,168
In the absence of serious maternal disorders, abortion procedures do not require hospitalization .

526
01:02:45,168 --> 01:02:52,806
However, outpatient facilities should be able to provide emergency resuscitation and immediate transfer to a hospital .

527
01:02:52,806 --> 01:02:55,431
# Surgical Abortion.

528
01:02:55,431 --> 01:03:02,993
Preoperative Preparation Surgical evacuation is performed transvaginally through an appropriately dilated cervix.

529
01:03:02,993 --> 01:03:18,031
For this, preoperative cervical ripening is typically associated with easier intraoperative cervical dilation, less pain, a technically easier procedure, and shorter operative times (Kapp, 2010; Webber, 2015).

530
01:03:18,031 --> 01:03:23,681
On balance, this preparation adds a surgical delay and potential side effects.

531
01:03:23,681 --> 01:03:30,968
Thus, cervical priming in the first trimester may be reserved for those with anticipated challenges to dilation.

532
01:03:30,968 --> 01:03:36,356
Examples are those with cervical stenosis or adolescents, who overall may

533
01:03:36,508 --> 01:03:39,558
experience higher associated pain .

534
01:03:39,508 --> 01:03:44,820
Surgical steps presented here apply to both induced abortion and miscarriage.

535
01:03:44,820 --> 01:03:56,520
For ripening, hygroscopic dilators, also called osmotic dilators, are devices that draw water from surrounding tissues and expand gradually to dilate the endocervical canal.

536
01:03:56,520 --> 01:04:02,933
One type is derived from various species of Laminaria algae that are harvested from the ocean floor .

537
01:04:02,933 --> 01:04:12,820
These come in different diameters, which allow the number and diameters of inserted devices, also called tents, to be customized to a given cervix.

538
01:04:12,820 --> 01:04:17,820
Another device is Dilapan-S, which is composed of an acrylic-based gel.

539
01:04:17,820 --> 01:04:23,533
Each type expands to an ultimate diameter three to four times that of its dry state.

540
01:04:23,533 --> 01:04:26,058
Figure 11-5.

541
01:04:26,058 --> 01:04:28,508
Hygroscopic dilators.

542
01:04:28,508 --> 01:04:36,883
With each type, the dry unit (left) expands exponentially when exposed to water (right) as in the endocervical canal.

543
01:04:36,883 --> 01:04:39,008
A. Laminaria.

544
01:04:39,008 --> 01:04:41,158
B. Dilapan-S.

545
01:04:41,158 --> 01:04:47,745
With hygroscopic dilators, shallow insertion yields insufficient dilation or tent expulsion.

546
01:04:47,745 --> 01:04:52,308
Overly deep placement risks dislodgement into the uterine cavity.

547
01:04:52,308 --> 01:04:59,883
Once tents are inserted, several gauze sponges placed at the external os help prevent spontaneous tent expulsion.

548
01:04:59,883 --> 01:05:04,445
Patients can ambulate, void, or stool without limitation.

549
01:05:04,445 --> 01:05:10,258
The numbers of sponges and dilators inserted are carefully counted and recorded in the chart.

550
01:05:10,258 --> 01:05:19,558
Schneider and coworkers (1991) described 21 cases in which women who had a hygroscopic dilator placed changed their minds.

551
01:05:19,558 --> 01:05:28,658
Of 17 women who chose to continue their pregnancy, 14 delivered at term, two delivered preterm, and one miscarried 2 weeks later.

552
01:05:28,658 --> 01:05:37,145
None suffered infection-related morbidity, including three untreated women with cervical cultures positive for Chlamydia trachomatis.

553
01:05:37,145 --> 01:05:41,745
Instead, misoprostol is often used for cervical ripening.

554
01:05:41,745 --> 01:05:52,358
The typical dose is 400 μg administered sublingually, buccally, or placed into the posterior vaginal fornix at least 3 to 4 hours prior to surgery.

555
01:05:52,358 --> 01:05:55,220
Oral ingestion is less effective .

556
01:05:55,220 --> 01:06:02,670
Another option is a 200-mg oral mifepristone dose given 24 to 48 hours before surgery .

557
01:06:02,670 --> 01:06:14,908
However, misoprostol is typically favored, because of mifepristone’s greater delay to the procedure, cost, and limited access within the FDA’s REMS program (p. 202).

558
01:06:14,908 --> 01:06:21,595
Of options, hygroscopic dilators provide equal or slightly greater dilation than misoprostol.

559
01:06:21,595 --> 01:06:29,545
Other surgical parameters do not vary significantly (Bartz, 2013; MacIsaac, 1999).

560
01:06:29,545 --> 01:06:42,670
On balance, the ripening time required for hygroscopic dilators extends the total procedure time and can be uncomfortable, whereas misoprostol can cause fever, bleeding, and gastrointestinal side effects.

561
01:06:42,670 --> 01:06:50,383
If not done as part of early prenatal care, hemoglobin level and Rh status are assessed prior to abortion.

562
01:06:50,383 --> 01:07:00,045
Screening for gonorrhea, for syphilis, and for human immunodeficiency virus, hepatitis B, and chlamydial infections also is completed.

563
01:07:00,045 --> 01:07:05,458
Obvious cervical infections are treated and resolved before elective procedures.

564
01:07:05,458 --> 01:07:16,958
To prevent postabortal infection after a first- or secondtrimester surgical evacuation, a 200-mg oral prophylactic dose of doxycycline is given 1 hour before.

565
01:07:16,958 --> 01:07:28,620
For those electing local anesthesia, evidence also supports adding an oral or intramuscular dose of a nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drug 30 to 60 minutes prior to surgery .

566
01:07:28,620 --> 01:07:42,833
Prophylaxis specifically for infective endocarditis prevention in those with valvular heart disease is not required in the absence of active infection (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020g,i).

567
01:07:42,833 --> 01:07:50,933
No recommendations specifically address venous thromboembolism prophylaxis for suction curettage in low-risk gravidas.

568
01:07:50,933 --> 01:07:54,795
At our hospital, we encourage early ambulation.

569
01:07:54,795 --> 01:08:07,745
Vacuum Aspiration Also called suction curettage or suction dilation and curettage, vacuum aspiration is a transcervical approach, in which the cervix is first dilated and tissue is then evacuated.

570
01:08:07,745 --> 01:08:17,020
For this, a rigid cannula is attached either to an electric-powered vacuum source or to a handheld 60-mL syringe for its vacuum source.

571
01:08:17,020 --> 01:08:24,383
These are electric vacuum aspiration (EVA) or manual vacuum aspiration (MVA), respectively.

572
01:08:24,383 --> 01:08:29,495
Sharp dilation and curettage (D & C) in which contents are

573
01:08:29,644 --> 01:08:45,481
mechanically scraped out solely by a sharp curette is currently not recommended for pregnancy evacuation due to greater blood loss, pain, and procedural time (National Abortion Federation, 2020; World Health Organization, 2012).

574
01:08:45,431 --> 01:08:52,494
Importantly, this practice is distinguished from brief final sharp curettage following initial aspiration.

575
01:08:52,494 --> 01:08:59,456
To begin, the surgeon performs a bimanual examination to confirm uterine size and orientation.

576
01:08:59,456 --> 01:09:05,956
A speculum is inserted, and the cervix is swabbed with povidone-iodine or equivalent solution.

577
01:09:05,956 --> 01:09:10,356
The anterior cervical lip is grasped with a toothed tenaculum.

578
01:09:10,356 --> 01:09:21,694
The cervix, vagina, and uterus are richly supplied by nerves of the Frankenhäuser plexus, which lies within connective tissue lateral to the uterosacral and cardinal ligaments.

579
01:09:21,694 --> 01:09:36,156
Thus, vacuum aspiration at minimum requires intravenously or orally administered sedatives or analgesics, and some add a paracervical or intracervical block (Allen, 2009; Renner, 2012).

580
01:09:36,156 --> 01:09:46,719
For a pudendal block, 5 mL of 1- or 2-percent lidocaine is injected into the uterosacral ligaments at their insertion into the uterus at 4 and 8 o’clock.

581
01:09:46,719 --> 01:09:58,069
Instead, an intracervical block with 5-mL aliquots of 1percent lidocaine injected at 12, 3, 6, and 9 o’clock was reported to be equally effective .

582
01:09:58,069 --> 01:10:02,006
General or regional anesthesia may instead be elected.

583
01:10:02,006 --> 01:10:08,681
First, a Sims uterine sound is passed into the uterus to measure the depth and inclination of the cavity.

584
01:10:08,681 --> 01:10:13,056
This provides parameters for subsequent instrument insertion.

585
01:10:13,056 --> 01:10:21,494
If required, the cervix is further dilated with Hegar, Hank, or Pratt dilators until a suction cannula can be inserted.

586
01:10:21,494 --> 01:10:28,394
As a rough rule, the degree of required cervical dilation in millimeters approximates gestational age.

587
01:10:28,394 --> 01:10:32,331
Hegar sizes reflect their diameter in millimeters.

588
01:10:32,331 --> 01:10:40,256
Pratt and Hank dilators are sized in French units, which can be converted to millimeters by dividing the French value by three.

589
01:10:40,256 --> 01:10:49,269
With dilation, the fourth and fifth fingers of the introducing hand should rest on the perineum and buttocks as the instrument is guided through the internal os .

590
01:10:49,269 --> 01:10:54,831
This technique minimizes forceful insertion and helps prevent uterine perforation.

591
01:10:54,831 --> 01:10:57,369
Figure 11-6.

592
01:10:57,369 --> 01:11:00,669
Dilation of cervix with a Hegar dilator.

593
01:11:00,669 --> 01:11:06,844
Note that the fourth and fifth fingers rest against the perineum and buttocks, lateral to the vagina.

594
01:11:06,844 --> 01:11:18,344
This maneuver is an important safety measure because if the cervix relaxes abruptly, these fingers prevent a sudden and uncontrolled thrust of the dilator, a common cause of uterine perforation.

595
01:11:18,344 --> 01:11:27,006
Following dilation, for most first-trimester aspiration procedures, an 8to 12-mm Karman cannula is appropriate.

596
01:11:27,006 --> 01:11:32,794
Small cannulas carry the risks of a longer surgery and of missed intrauterine tissue.

597
01:11:32,794 --> 01:11:37,081
Large cannulas risk cervical injury and more discomfort.

598
01:11:37,081 --> 01:11:43,256
For evacuation, the cannula is slowly moved toward the fundus until resistance is met.

599
01:11:43,256 --> 01:11:45,744
Suction is then activated.

600
01:11:45,744 --> 01:11:55,069
The cannula is gradually pulled back toward the os and is simultaneously slowly turned circumferentially to cover the entire uterine cavity surface .

601
01:11:55,069 --> 01:11:58,894
This is repeated until no more tissue is aspirated.

602
01:11:58,894 --> 01:12:04,106
A gentle sharp curettage can follow to remove any remaining tissue fragments .

603
01:12:04,106 --> 01:12:12,606
Strong and consistent evidence supports the high efficacy, safety, and patient acceptability for both MVA and EVA .

604
01:12:12,606 --> 01:12:15,069
Figure 11-7.

605
01:12:15,069 --> 01:12:19,494
A suction curette has been placed through the cervix into the uterus.

606
01:12:19,494 --> 01:12:23,994
The figure shows the rotary motion used to aspirate the contents.

607
01:12:23,994 --> 01:12:35,044
Figures 11-7 and 11-8: Reproduced with permission from Hoffman BL, Hamid CA, Corton MM: Surgeries for benign gynecologic disorders.

608
01:12:35,044 --> 01:12:42,631
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al (eds): Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

609
01:12:42,631 --> 01:12:46,556
New York, McGraw Hill Education, 2020.

610
01:12:46,556 --> 01:12:48,819
Figure 11-8.

611
01:12:48,819 --> 01:12:57,306
A sharp curette is advanced into the uterine cavity while the instrument is held with the thumb and forefinger as shown in Figure 11-6.

612
01:12:57,306 --> 01:13:02,581
Upon reaching the fundus, the curette is placed flush against the uterine wall.

613
01:13:02,581 --> 01:13:08,931
Firm pressure against the wall as the instrument is dragged out will scrape away adhered tissue fragments.

614
01:13:08,931 --> 01:13:14,756
With movement of the curette, only the strength of these two fingers should be used to help avoid

615
01:13:14,908 --> 01:13:16,708
perforation.

616
01:13:16,658 --> 01:13:25,295
For abortion done at ≤6 weeks’ gestation, a distinct drawback is that the pregnancy may be small and missed by the curette.

617
01:13:25,295 --> 01:13:36,195
To identify placenta, the aspirated contents are rinsed in a strainer to remove blood, and then placed in a clear plastic container with saline and examined with back lighting .

618
01:13:36,195 --> 01:13:39,945
Placental tissue macroscopically appears feathery.

619
01:13:39,945 --> 01:13:44,970
A magnifying lens, colposcope, or microscope can augment viewing.

620
01:13:44,970 --> 01:13:50,345
With gestations <6 weeks, the failed abortion rate approximates 2 percent .

621
01:13:50,345 --> 01:13:57,558
Thus, if products are not clearly identified, serial serum β-hCG levels can be informative .

622
01:13:57,558 --> 01:14:11,495
Abortion Complications Legally induced abortion in the United States has a low associated mortality rate, and from 2013 to 2017, the rate was 0.4 deaths in 100,000 procedures .

623
01:14:11,495 --> 01:14:13,908
Early abortions are safer.

624
01:14:13,908 --> 01:14:28,445
The mortality rate was 0.3 deaths in 100,000 procedures performed ≤8 weeks’ gestation but rose to 2.5 at 14 to 17 weeks and to 6.7 at ≥18 weeks .

625
01:14:28,445 --> 01:14:34,483
Notably, maternal mortality rates are 14-fold greater for pregnancies that are continued .

626
01:14:34,483 --> 01:14:43,645
Uterine perforation and lower-genital-tract laceration are uncommon but potentially serious, and rates also rise with gestational age.

627
01:14:43,645 --> 01:14:52,870
In one systematic review of first-trimester abortion, the uterine perforation and laceration rates were each ≤1 percent .

628
01:14:52,870 --> 01:14:59,170
Perforation is usually recognized when the instrument passes without resistance deep into the pelvis.

629
01:14:59,170 --> 01:15:12,870
Risk factors include operator inexperience, prior cervical surgery or anomaly, adolescence, multiparity, and advanced gestational age (Allen, 2016; Grimes, 1984).

630
01:15:12,870 --> 01:15:24,333
If the uterine perforation is small and fundal, as when produced by a uterine sound or narrow dilator, observation for vital sign changes or uterine bleeding is usually sufficient.

631
01:15:24,333 --> 01:15:32,608
If a suction cannula or sharp curette passes into the peritoneal cavity, considerable intraabdominal damage can ensue.

632
01:15:32,608 --> 01:15:39,495
Laparotomy or laparoscopy to thoroughly examine the abdominal contents is often the safest course.

633
01:15:39,495 --> 01:15:49,245
After potential injuries are resolved, intraoperatively, curettage can be completed under the direct guidance provided by laparoscopy or laparotomy .

634
01:15:49,245 --> 01:15:56,608
Following curettage, uterine synechiae may form, and the risk of synechiae increases with procedure number.

635
01:15:56,608 --> 01:16:01,295
Most cases are mild and of unclear reproductive significance .

636
01:16:01,295 --> 01:16:09,058
However, of Asherman syndrome cases, one series found that two thirds were linked to firsttrimester curettage .

637
01:16:09,058 --> 01:16:16,120
Other first-trimester abortion complications are germane to both surgical and medication abortion techniques.

638
01:16:16,120 --> 01:16:20,070
First, hemorrhage with abortion is variably defined.

639
01:16:20,070 --> 01:16:28,470
One supported by the Society for Family Planning is bleeding that prompts a clinical response or bleeding in excess of 500 mL .

640
01:16:28,470 --> 01:16:34,958
For first-trimester surgical abortions, hemorrhage complicates ≤1 percent .

641
01:16:34,958 --> 01:16:43,008
Atony, abnormal placentation, and coagulopathy are frequent sources, whereas surgical trauma is a rare cause.

642
01:16:43,008 --> 01:16:46,745
With medication abortion, bleeding is more common.

643
01:16:46,745 --> 01:17:02,858
In one study of more than 42,000 Finnish women undergoing pregnancy termination with pregnancies less 63 days, hemorrhage complicated 15 percent of medication abortion but only 2 percent of surgical cases (Niinimäki, 2009).

644
01:17:02,858 --> 01:17:05,283
Infection is another risk.

645
01:17:05,283 --> 01:17:14,458
One review found a cumulative rate of 0.5 percent in those given prophylaxis compared with 2.6 percent in those given placebo .

646
01:17:14,458 --> 01:17:22,458
In another review, the postoperative infection rate was <0.3 percent for either surgical or medication abortion .

647
01:17:22,458 --> 01:17:26,220
Incomplete abortion may require reevacuation.

648
01:17:26,220 --> 01:17:31,595
For medication abortion, this neared 5 percent in one systematic review .

649
01:17:31,595 --> 01:17:41,170
Reaspiration rates following surgical abortion are typically <2 percent (Ireland, 2015; Niinimaki, 2009).

650
01:17:41,170 --> 01:17:52,220
In sum, surgical abortion offers higher efficacy rates (96 to 100 percent) than medication abortion (83 to 98 percent) in the first trimester.

651
01:17:52,220 --> 01:17:59,320
Medication abortion also carries a greater cumulative risk of complications, although differences are small .

652
01:17:59,320 --> 01:18:06,145
These are balanced against the greater privacy of medication abortion and the more invasive steps of curettage.

653
01:18:06,145 --> 01:18:08,870
# Medication Abortion.

654
01:18:08,870 --> 01:18:12,220
Agents Used For many women, outpatient

655
01:18:12,388 --> 01:18:19,950
medication abortion is an acceptable option for pregnancies with a menstrual age ≤70 days.

656
01:18:19,900 --> 01:18:25,688
Although suitable at later gestational ages, successful evacuation rates are lower.

657
01:18:25,688 --> 01:18:35,625
Among abortions performed at ≤9 weeks’ gestation, 39 percent were completed with medication in the United States in 2018 .

658
01:18:35,625 --> 01:18:42,263
One regimen provides mifepristone plus misoprostol, but another provides misoprostol alone.

659
01:18:42,263 --> 01:18:50,113
Of actions, mifepristone augments uterine contractility by reversing progesterone-induced myometrial quiescence.

660
01:18:50,113 --> 01:18:54,063
Misoprostol directly stimulates the myometrium.

661
01:18:54,063 --> 01:19:00,538
Both also ripen the cervix (Mahajan, 1997; Tang, 2007).

662
01:19:00,538 --> 01:19:06,300
Contraindications have evolved from exclusion criteria that were used in clinical trials.

663
01:19:06,300 --> 01:19:23,913
Cautions include suspected ectopic pregnancy; in-situ intrauterine device; severe anemia, coagulopathy, or anticoagulant use; longterm systemic corticosteroid therapy; chronic adrenal failure; inherited porphyria; or allergy to agents used .

664
01:19:23,913 --> 01:19:30,588
Of note, misoprostol is suitable for early pregnancy failure in those with prior uterine surgery .

665
01:19:30,588 --> 01:19:40,025
Misoprostol is a teratogen that is associated with transverse limb reduction or with the Mobius sequence, which is manifest by cranial nerve palsy.

666
01:19:40,025 --> 01:19:45,388
Thus, a commitment to abortion completion is essential once this drug is given .

667
01:19:45,388 --> 01:19:54,850
With mifepristone, for women who choose to continue their pregnancies after exposure, the ongoing pregnancy rate ranges from 10 to 46 percent .

668
01:19:54,850 --> 01:20:01,875
The associated major malformation rate was 5 percent in one series of 46 exposed pregnancies .

669
01:20:01,875 --> 01:20:05,600
Administration Several dosing schemes are effective .

670
01:20:05,600 --> 01:20:11,850
Because of its greater efficacy, mifepristone plus misoprostol combinations are favored.

671
01:20:11,850 --> 01:20:21,788
For gestations ≤70 days, the most widely accepted regimen is mifepristone, 200 mg given orally on day 0.

672
01:20:21,788 --> 01:20:31,200
This is followed in 24 to 48 hours by an 800-μg misoprostol dose that is administered vaginally, buccally, or sublingually .

673
01:20:31,200 --> 01:20:34,600
Oral ingestion has lower efficacy rates.

674
01:20:34,600 --> 01:20:40,000
If desired, mifepristone and misoprostol may be self-administered at home .

675
01:20:40,000 --> 01:20:48,338
Associated infection rates approximate 0.3 percent, and antibiotic prophylaxis is not currently recommended .

676
01:20:48,338 --> 01:20:50,800
Table 11-6.

677
01:20:50,800 --> 01:21:01,538
Various Regimens for Medical Termination of Pregnancy Symptoms following misoprostol are common within 3 hours and include vomiting, diarrhea, fever, and chills.

678
01:21:01,538 --> 01:21:07,725
Bleeding and cramping with medication abortion typically is significantly worse than that with menses.

679
01:21:07,725 --> 01:21:10,963
Thus, oral analgesics are provided.

680
01:21:10,963 --> 01:21:18,375
If bleeding soaks two or more pads in an hour for at least 2 hours, the woman is instructed to contact her provider.

681
01:21:18,375 --> 01:21:25,938
Reappointment is 1 to 2 weeks following drug administration, and bimanual pelvic examination is recommended.

682
01:21:25,938 --> 01:21:30,375
Abortion completion may be assessed with β-hCG levels.

683
01:21:30,375 --> 01:21:36,950
Expected rates of decline at 1 week can be extrapolated from Table 11-1 and can guide care.

684
01:21:36,950 --> 01:21:43,925
However, specific β-hCG values lack suitable sensitivity (Rørbye, 2004).

685
01:21:43,925 --> 01:21:49,938
Moreover, routine postabortal sonographic examination is typically unnecessary .

686
01:21:49,938 --> 01:21:54,863
If done, it performs comparably to β-hCG level assessment .

687
01:21:54,863 --> 01:22:04,050
If sonography is done due to concern for failed abortion or for bleeding, unnecessary surgery can be avoided if scans are interpreted appropriately.

688
01:22:04,050 --> 01:22:10,438
If no gestational sac is seen and no heavy bleeding is noted, intervention is unnecessary.

689
01:22:10,438 --> 01:22:16,800
This is true even when, as is common, the uterus contains sonographically evident debris .

690
01:22:16,800 --> 01:22:19,238
Second-Trimester Methods.

691
01:22:19,238 --> 01:22:30,650
In the second trimester, fetal anomaly or death, maternal health complications, inevitable abortion, or desired termination may be indications for uterine evacuation.

692
01:22:30,650 --> 01:22:35,550
As in the first trimester, medication or surgery is an option.

693
01:22:35,550 --> 01:22:45,163
In the second trimester, dilation and evacuation (D & E) rather than suction D & C is needed because of larger fetal size and bones.

694
01:22:45,163 --> 01:22:49,663
Of options, D & E is a common means in the United States.

695
01:22:49,663 --> 01:22:58,563
Of all induced abortions in 2018, nearly 8 percent were performed by D & E at gestational ages >13 weeks .

696
01:22:58,563 --> 01:23:07,200
Many of the surgical and medication steps for second-trimester abortion mirror those in the first trimester, and differences are emphasized here.

697
01:23:07,200 --> 01:23:09,625
# Dilation and

698
01:23:09,796 --> 01:23:11,708
Evacuation.

699
01:23:11,658 --> 01:23:18,796
Preparation With D & E, wide mechanical cervical dilation is needed for evacuation of fetal parts.

700
01:23:18,796 --> 01:23:28,121
The degree needed rises with fetal gestational age, and inadequate dilation risks cervical trauma, uterine perforation, or tissue retention .

701
01:23:28,121 --> 01:23:37,671
Thus, presurgical cervical preparation is advised, and main options include hygroscopic dilators or misoprostol (p. 209).

702
01:23:37,671 --> 01:23:43,158
With laminaria, overnight preparation offers optimal cervical dilation .

703
01:23:43,158 --> 01:23:53,158
Uncommonly, laminaria may fail to adequately dilate the cervix, and serial insertions over several days with an increasing number of tents is an option.

704
01:23:53,158 --> 01:23:55,808
Dilapan-S also is suitable.

705
01:23:55,808 --> 01:24:03,096
It may be preferable for same-day procedures, as this device achieves its maximal effect in 4 to 6 hours .

706
01:24:03,096 --> 01:24:13,683
Supplementing laminaria with mifepristone can aid procedures for gestations >19 weeks (Diedrich, 2020; Goldberg, 2015).

707
01:24:13,683 --> 01:24:21,646
In contrast, misoprostol supplementation to hygroscopic dilators failed to add benefits in one metaanalysis .

708
01:24:21,646 --> 01:24:30,508
If a patient changes abortion plans after hygroscopic dilator removal, preterm delivery and PPROM rates are substantial.

709
01:24:30,508 --> 01:24:36,971
In one series of 12 such cases, 50 percent ended with miscarriage or perinatal death .

710
01:24:36,971 --> 01:24:41,183
Misoprostol alone can be used for cervical preparation.

711
01:24:41,183 --> 01:25:01,658
The typical dose is 400 μg given vaginally or buccally 3 to 4 hours prior to D & E. Randomized trial results vary regarding the ability of misoprostol to achieve results equal to that with hydroscopic dilators (Bartz, 2013; Goldberg, 2005; Sagiv, 2015).

712
01:25:01,658 --> 01:25:15,221
In women with one prior hysterotomy, the risk of uterine rupture was not elevated by misoprostol cervical ripening in one review but rose to 2.5 percent in those with ≥2 cesarean deliveries .

713
01:25:15,221 --> 01:25:20,433
With mifepristone alone for cervical ripening, fewer studies provide data.

714
01:25:20,433 --> 01:25:26,246
In one, mifepristone alone provided less dilation than hydroscopic dilators .

715
01:25:26,246 --> 01:25:35,358
In another trial, mifepristone given 48 hours before misoprostol created greater cervical dilation compared with misoprostol alone .

716
01:25:35,358 --> 01:25:50,121
In sum, hygroscopic dilators soften and dilate the cervix before D & E. Sequential insertions or layering agents may be most helpful for later gestations or for an inadequate response to initial hygroscopic dilators alone.

717
01:25:50,121 --> 01:25:54,208
Yet, layering adds cost and potential side effects.

718
01:25:54,208 --> 01:26:01,033
With elective abortion, some choose to induce fetal demise prior to D & E to avert a live birth.

719
01:26:01,033 --> 01:26:11,408
For this, an intracardiac potassium chloride or lidocaine injection or an intraamnionic or intrafetal digoxin injection is used prior to cervical ripening .

720
01:26:11,408 --> 01:26:19,271
Technique During D & E, sonography can be used as an adjunct in all cases or selectively in more challenging ones.

721
01:26:19,271 --> 01:26:26,508
Perioperative antibiotic prophylaxis mirrors that for first-trimester procedures (p. 209).

722
01:26:26,508 --> 01:26:38,308
To reduce postprocedure bleeding, dilute vasopressin can be injected intracervically or as part of a paracervical block (Kerns, 2013; Schulz, 1985).

723
01:26:38,308 --> 01:26:49,033
Once adequate cervical dilation is achieved, the initial surgical step drains amnionic fluid with an 11- to 16-mm suction cannula or with amniotomy.

724
01:26:49,033 --> 01:27:00,271
This reduces the risk of amnionic fluid embolism and brings the fetus into the lower uterine segment for removal (Owen, 2017; Prager, 2009).

725
01:27:00,271 --> 01:27:09,958
For pregnancies >16 weeks’ gestation, the fetus is extracted, often in parts, using Sopher forceps or other destructive instruments.

726
01:27:09,958 --> 01:27:16,108
With complete removal of the fetus, a large-bore vacuum curette is used to remove the placenta.

727
01:27:16,108 --> 01:27:27,158
Major complications are infrequent with D & E, and rates range from 1 to 2 percent in large series (Lederle, 2015; Peterson, 1983).

728
01:27:27,158 --> 01:27:34,333
These include uterine perforation, cervical laceration, uterine bleeding, and postabortal infection.

729
01:27:34,333 --> 01:27:43,658
Prior cesarean delivery is not a contraindication for D & E and may be preferred over prostaglandins for those with multiple prior hysterotomies .

730
01:27:43,658 --> 01:27:51,708
Other Surgical Considerations Placenta previa or the placenta accreta syndrome (PAS) can raise D & E risks.

731
01:27:51,708 --> 01:27:56,008
Once diagnosed, PAS typically prompts hysterectomy .

732
01:27:56,008 --> 01:28:05,371
For placenta previa, D & E is preferred to quickly evacuate the placenta, but the ability to transfuse blood products and perform possible

733
01:28:05,524 --> 01:28:15,111
hysterectomy must be available (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019f; Perriera, 2017).

734
01:28:15,061 --> 01:28:21,286
Medication abortion may be elected, but the risk for transfusion is greater than with D & E .

735
01:28:21,286 --> 01:28:28,211
Data are conflicting regarding the value of predelivery uterine artery embolization to lessen bleeding risks .

736
01:28:28,211 --> 01:28:34,649
In some cases of failed second-trimester medication abortion, hysterotomy may be considered.

737
01:28:34,649 --> 01:28:44,174
In other cases, if comorbid uterine pathology is significant, such as numerous large myomas, hysterectomy may provide ideal treatment.

738
01:28:44,174 --> 01:28:50,174
Some women with second-trimester pregnancies desire sterilization following evacuation.

739
01:28:50,174 --> 01:28:58,449
Because the contracted uterine fundus will lie lower, any laparotomy incision must be placed to allow fallopian tube access.

740
01:28:58,449 --> 01:29:01,174
# Medication Abortion.

741
01:29:01,174 --> 01:29:08,024
Principal among noninvasive methods is a mifepristone plus misoprostol regimen or misoprostol alone .

742
01:29:08,024 --> 01:29:16,924
Of these two options, the combined regimen yields a shorter termination duration (Kapp, 2007; Ngoc, 2011).

743
01:29:16,924 --> 01:29:26,211
Hygroscopic dilators may speed the time to delivery with this combined regimen (Mazouni, 2009; Vincienne, 2018).

744
01:29:26,211 --> 01:29:34,774
In selecting misoprostol routes, oral administration leads to a longer time to delivery compared with vaginal or sublingual routes .

745
01:29:34,774 --> 01:29:41,986
Prophylactic antibiotics are not typically given, and infection surveillance during labor is instead applied .

746
01:29:41,986 --> 01:29:53,199
Another induction agent, PGE2, shows similar efficacy and side effects compared with misoprostol (Jain, 1994; Jansen, 2008).

747
01:29:53,199 --> 01:30:07,986
Simultaneous administration of an antiemetic such as metoclopramide (Reglan), an antipyretic such as acetaminophen, and an antidiarrheal such as diphenoxylate/atropine (Lomotil) will help prevent or treat symptoms.

748
01:30:07,986 --> 01:30:13,661
Dinoprostone (Prostin) is an available PGE2 in the United States.

749
01:30:13,661 --> 01:30:21,374
However, its greater cost and poor pharmacologic stability at room temperature may make it less attractive than misoprostol.

750
01:30:21,374 --> 01:30:30,699
During medication abortion, the uterine rupture rate is 0.4 percent with misoprostol and one prior low transverse cesarean delivery .

751
01:30:30,699 --> 01:30:39,324
Both misoprostol and PGE2 appear to pose similar risk (le Roux, 2001; Reichman, 2007).

752
01:30:39,324 --> 01:30:46,386
Few data guide their use for medication abortion in those with ≥2 prior cesarean deliveries.

753
01:30:46,386 --> 01:30:55,424
Of other agents, high-dose intravenous oxytocin in saline will result in second-trimester abortion in 80 to 90 percent of cases .

754
01:30:55,424 --> 01:31:02,936
However, by comparison, misoprostol leads to higher successful induction rates and faster delivery times .

755
01:31:02,936 --> 01:31:12,686
Rarely used in the United States, ethacridine lactate is an organic antiseptic that activates myometrial mast cells to release prostaglandins .

756
01:31:12,686 --> 01:31:21,424
The solution is instilled either intraamnionically or extraovularly, that is, into the potential space between the uterine wall and amnion.

757
01:31:21,424 --> 01:31:27,999
Compared with misoprostol, it is associated with longer times to delivery and lower success rates .

758
01:31:27,999 --> 01:31:31,474
# Fetal and Placental Evaluation.

759
01:31:31,474 --> 01:31:40,499
For second-trimester pregnancies being terminated for maternal health or fetal indications, either D & E or medication abortion is suitable.

760
01:31:40,499 --> 01:31:45,499
Thus, patient input and clinical indication both guide selection.

761
01:31:45,499 --> 01:31:50,261
Perinatal palliative care can be instituted (p. 203) .

762
01:31:50,261 --> 01:31:56,911
Evaluation of a stillborn fetus is described in Chapter 35 (p. 626).

763
01:31:56,911 --> 01:32:08,024
One component is autopsy, which can also be valuable for second-trimester terminations due to anomalies (Hauerberg, 2012; Man, 2016).

764
01:32:08,024 --> 01:32:13,724
Fragmented D & E specimens may provide less information than intact fetuses .

765
01:32:13,724 --> 01:32:16,624
Elective Abortion Consequences.

766
01:32:16,624 --> 01:32:22,936
Data relating abortion to overall maternal health and to subsequent pregnancy outcome are limited.

767
01:32:22,936 --> 01:32:31,174
Rates of infertility or ectopic pregnancy are not increased (Burkman, 1988; Frank, 1993).

768
01:32:31,174 --> 01:32:37,549
Exceptions may stem from postabortal infections, especially those caused by C trachomatis.

769
01:32:37,549 --> 01:32:52,286
Of subsequent adverse pregnancy outcomes, several studies note an approximate 1.5-fold greater incidence of preterm delivery following surgical evacuation (Lemmers, 2016; Saccone, 2016).

770
01:32:52,286 --> 01:32:55,886
This risk accrues with the number of terminations .

771
01:32:55,886 --> 01:33:06,049
Subsequent pregnancy outcomes are similar if a prior induced abortion was completed with medication or by surgery (Männistö, 2013; Virk,

772
01:33:06,220 --> 01:33:09,957
2007). Postabortal Contraception.

773
01:33:09,907 --> 01:33:20,307
After early abortion, woman may ovulate after 8 days, but the average is 3 weeks (Lahteenmaki, 1978; Stoddard, 2011).

774
01:33:20,307 --> 01:33:29,295
Unless another pregnancy is imminently desired, contraception, either hormonal or nonhormonal, can be started immediately after abortion .

775
01:33:29,295 --> 01:33:44,670
In candidates, defined in Chapter 38 (p. 667), an intrauterine device can be inserted immediately after surgery or medication abortion is completed (Bednarek, 2011; Korjamo, 2017).

776
01:33:44,670 --> 01:33:49,745
For women who desire another pregnancy, conception need not be delayed.

777
01:33:49,745 --> 01:33:59,407
From studies, groups conceiving within 3 months of a first-trimester loss had lower subsequent miscarriage rates compared with groups with later conceptions .

